We need two quantitative consequences of this construction. Let $k_y$ be a normalized coherent orbital centered at $y$.

#thm([Lemma 3.1.])[
  (Uniform frame localization).  #emph[For every $D$ there is $C_D$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.7)")
 $ \| chevron.l z_x \, k_y chevron.r \| lt.eq C_D \( 1 + d \( x \, y \) \)^(- D) . $ <eq:frame-coherent-decay>]

 For two frames constructed as above, possibly with different axes,
   their atoms satisfy the same bound with $k_y$ replaced by an atom centered at $y$.
   For each fixed integer $J$, (3.7) also holds, with constants depending on $J$,
   for every rotated polar orbital $e_j \( y \)$ with $0 lt.eq j lt.eq J$.]
] <locality:frame>

#proof[
  Choose the axial gauge and azimuth convention in which the coefficient of $e_j$ in $k_y$ is $e^(i j phi.alt_y)
    p_(q \, m) \( j \)^(1 \/ 2)$. Its modulus is
  $ p_(q \, m) \( j \)^(1 \/ 2) \, wide p_(q \, m) \( j \) = binom(q, j) \( m \/ q \)^j \( 1 - m \/ q \)^(q - j) . $
  They obey, after extension by zero outside $\[ 0 \, q \]$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.8)")
   $ \| Delta^l p_(q \, m) \( j \)^(1 \/ 2) \| lt.eq C_(D \, l) w_q \( m \)^(- 1 \/ 2 - l)
     (1 + frac(\| j - m \|, w_q \( m \)))^(- D) . $ <eq:binomial-symbol>]
  Here and below finite differences may shift the argument by a fixed number of indices,
    which only changes the constant. We include a verification to specify uniformity near the ends.
    First put $p = m \/ q$ and $sigma^2 = q p \( 1 - p \)$, so $w_q \( m \)^2 = 1 +
    q sigma^2 \/ \( q + 1 \) asymp 1 + sigma^2$. Fourier inversion of a binomial law of variance $sigma^2$ gives
  $ sup_j p_(q \, m) \( j \) lt.eq frac(1, 2 pi) integral_(- pi)^pi #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 -
    4 p \( 1 - p \) sin^2 \( t \/ 2 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(q \/ 2) thin d t lt.eq frac(C, 1 + sigma) . $
  For $0 < p < 1$, tilt the law by $e^(lambda j)$. Its new parameter is $p_lambda =
    p e^lambda \/ \( 1 - p + p e^lambda \)$, and its variance differs from $sigma^2$ by a factor between
    $e^(- \| lambda \|)$ and $e^(\| lambda \|)$. Therefore the centered log moment generating function satisfies
    $log bb(E) e^(lambda \( X - m \)) lt.eq sigma^2 lambda^2$ for $\| lambda \| lt.eq 1 \/ 2$.
    At $z = j - m$, choose $lambda = z \/ \( 2 \( sigma^2 + \| z \| \) \)$.
    The exact tilting identity and the preceding point-mass bound give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.9)")
   $ p_(q \, m) \( j \) lt.eq frac(C, w_q \( m \)) exp (- frac(c \| j - m \|^2, w_q \( m \)^2 + \| j - m \|)) .
     $ <eq:binomial-global-tail>]
  The cases $p = 0 \, 1$ are direct. This proves every polynomial tail bound with the required height,
    uniformly also when $w_q \( m \)$ is bounded.
  For the derivative gain first assume $w_q \( m \)^2 gt.eq 16 \( l + 1 \)$, and let $h =
    min \( m \, q - m \) asymp w_q \( m \)^2$. Interpolate the square root of the binomial probability by gamma
    functions, writing the resulting positive function as $F \( x \)$.
    Put $psi = \( log Gamma \)'$ and $psi_1 = \( log Gamma \)''$. On $\| x - m \| lt.eq h \/ 2$, the explicit formulas
  $ \( log F \)' \( x \) = 1 / 2 (log frac(p, 1 - p) - psi \( x + 1 \) + psi \( q - x + 1 \)) \,\
\( log F \)'' \( x \) = - 1 / 2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] psi_1 \( x + 1 \) +
  psi_1 \( q - x + 1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $
  and the positive-real polygamma series give $\| \( log F \)' \( m \) \| lt.eq C w^(- 2)$,
    $\( log F \)'' \( x \) lt.eq - c w^(- 2)$, and $\| \( log F \)^(\( k \)) \( x \) \| lt.eq
    C_k w^(- k)$ for $k gt.eq 2$. Stirling’s inequalities give $F \( m \) lt.eq C w^(- 1 \/ 2)$.
    Differentiating $F = e^(log F)$ consequently proves
  $ \| F^(\( l \)) \( x \) \| lt.eq C_(D \, l) w^(- 1 \/ 2 - l) \( 1 + \| x - m \| \/ w \)^(- D) \, wide \| x -
    m \| lt.eq h \/ 2 . $
  If $\| j - m \| lt.eq h \/ 4$, integrate this derivative over the unit cube that represents $Delta^l F \( j \)$;
    the entire stencil stays in the displayed interval. Outside that region,
    each nonzero stencil entry has $\| j + a - m \| gt.eq c w^2$, $0 lt.eq a lt.eq l$.
    Equation (3.9) then supplies an exponential in that distance,
    which pays both $w^(- l)$ and every polynomial tail. This also handles stencils crossing an endpoint.
    Finally, if $w^2 < 16 \( l + 1 \)$, bound the finite difference by its $l + 1$ values and use (3.9);
    the factor $w^(- l)$ is now a constant depending only on $l$.
    This completes the proof of (3.8), including the bounded-width regime.
  Insert (3.8) in (3.4). For windows whose radial centers are a bounded distance apart,
    their widths are comparable; summation by parts $l$ times gives the factor
    $\( 1 + w_a \| phi.alt_x - phi.alt_y \| \)^(- l)$, with angular difference modulo $2 pi$.
    The absolute sum before summation by parts is bounded. For separated radial windows,
    the tail in (3.8) first supplies arbitrary powers of radial distance.
    The ratios of the widths cost only polynomial powers by (3.2).
    Spending additional tail orders therefore gives the same angular estimate.
    Radial distance plus the shorter latitude arc bounds the spherical distance from above, proving (3.7).
  The coherent resolution of the identity has density $\( q + 1 \) \/ \( 2 pi q \)$ with respect to area.
    Inserting it between two frame atoms reduces the frame-to-frame bound to
  $ integral_(S_q) \( 1 + d \( x \, z \) \)^(- D - 3) \( 1 + d \( z \, y \) \)^(- D - 3) thin d z lt.eq
    C_D \( 1 + d \( x \, y \) \)^(- D) . $
  The inequality follows by dividing the integral into the sets where $d \( x \, z \) gt.eq
    d \( x \, y \) \/ 2$ and where $d \( z \, y \) gt.eq d \( x \, y \) \/ 2$. Finally
  $ \| chevron.l e_j \( y \) \, k_z chevron.r \|^2 = binom(q, j) sin^(2 j) \( theta.alt \/ 2 \)
    cos^(2 \( q - j \)) \( theta.alt \/ 2 \) \, $
  where $theta.alt$ is the angle between $y$ and $z$. For bounded $j$,
    this is bounded by $C_j \( 1 + d \( y \, z \) \)^(2 j) e^(- c d \( y \, z \)^2)$;
    increasing the constant covers bounded $q$. Inserting the coherent resolution once more proves the last assertion. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 3.2.])[
  (Common frames for nearby fluxes).  #emph[For each fixed $d_0$, frames at fluxes $q$ and $t$ with $0 lt.eq q -
    t lt.eq d_0$ may be embedded in the same slots so that index identification $i_(q \, t) :
    e_j^(\( t \)) mapsto e_j^(\( q \))$, $j lt.eq t$, satisfies the exact identity $sans(S)_t =
    sans(S)_q i_(q \, t)$. All preceding estimates remain uniform.
    In the northern third the windows can be chosen from one system independent of the flux;
    there one may use the common metric with radial variable $sqrt(1 + m)$.]
] <locality:common-frames>

#proof[
  Use the windows and Fourier lengths at flux $q$ for both spaces, and restrict their index sequences to $0 lt.eq
    j lt.eq t$ for the smaller space. Equation (3.5) still holds on those indices.
    Only a bounded number of end indices have been removed. The windows meeting them have bounded width,
    or have rapidly small tails in the estimates in which an endpoint extension occurs.
    The slot positions for the two sphere metrics differ by a bounded distance; the constants may depend on $d_0$.
    For all sufficiently large $q$, every window centered in the northern third has its full support below $q \/
    2$, because its width is $O \( sqrt(q) \)$. On these supports the construction uses only
    $eta \( sqrt(1 + j) - k \)$ and their fixed normalization,
    and their Fourier length is the fixed integer $16 \( k + 1 \)$.
    These complete slot vectors are therefore independent of $q$ in index coordinates.
    The buffer between $q \/ 3$ and $q \/ 2$ permits changing the frame outside this region without changing its
    northern slots. Bounded fluxes may use pairwise padded frames; no long sequence of common slots is needed there. ◻
]

Whenever operators at these two fluxes are subtracted, we first use $i_(q \, t)$ and extend the smaller operator
  by the identity on the added orbital modes. Thus, under the graded decomposition $cal(F)_q =
  cal(F)_t thin hat(⊗) thin and.big "span" { e_(t + 1) \, dots.h \, e_q }$,
  the padded operator is $A accent(⊗ I, ̂)$. Extra modes are put in their vacuum only when comparing states or
  taking the physical compression.

The fermionic Fock space over $ell^2 \( Lambda_q \)$ is a graded tensor product of two-dimensional slot spaces.
  The exterior isometry $Gamma \( sans(S)_q \)$ identifies $cal(F)_q$ with the subspace in which the orthogonal
  complement of $sans(S)_q U_q$ is vacant. A #emph[physical] operator is an operator in the CAR algebra generated
  by $sans(S)_q U_q$, extended as the identity on the complementary modes.
  It preserves that vacant-complement subspace. For $X subset Lambda_q$,
  let $frak(A) \( X \)$ be the CAR algebra of its slots. Put $cal(N)_(upright("slot")) =
  sum_(x in Lambda_q) c_x^(*) c_x$. An operator has number grade $k in
  bb(Z)$ if $\[ cal(N)_(upright("slot")) \, A \] = k A$, and parity $epsilon.alt_A in { 0 \,
  1 }$ if $\( - 1 \)^(cal(N)_(upright("slot"))) A \( - 1 \)^(cal(N)_(upright("slot"))) = \( - 1 \)^(epsilon.alt_A)
  A$. Operators of even parity on disjoint sets commute; homogeneous operators obey the graded relation

$ \[ A \, B \]_(upright("gr")) = A B - \( - 1 \)^(epsilon.alt_A epsilon.alt_B) B A = 0 . $

All Hamiltonians below have even parity. Local expansions can be projected onto any prescribed number grade by
  averaging the gauge action of the total slot number $cal(N)_(upright("slot"))$.
  This action preserves every slot algebra and does not increase approximation errors.
  On physical operators its grading agrees with that of the physical number operator $cal(N)$.

=== 3.2 Local norms and summation <local-norms-and-summation>

For a center $x$, an operator $A$, and $l > 0$, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.10)")
 $ parallel A parallel_(x \, l) = parallel A parallel + sup_(r gt.eq 1) \( 1 + r \)^l
   inf_(A_r in frak(A) \( B_r \( x \) \)) parallel A - A_r parallel . $ <locality:ball-norm>]

We call $A$ rapidly local at $x$ if this is finite for every $l$,
  with bounds uniform in the volume under consideration. An interaction is a family $A_x$ integrated against a
  measure whose mass in every unit ball is bounded. A bound of #emph[size] $a \( x \)$ means $parallel A_x
  parallel_(x \, l) lt.eq C_l a \( x \)$ for every $l$. A positive function $a$ is called tempered if,
  for fixed $C \, kappa$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.11)")
 $ a \( x \) lt.eq C a \( y \) \( 1 + d \( x \, y \) \)^kappa . $ <eq:tempered-weight>]

The constants in estimates involving $a$ may depend on $C \, kappa$. Powers and products of $u \, v \, w$ are examples.

Taking near-best approximations at radii $2^k$ and subtracting successive ones gives a ball decomposition

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.12)")
 $ A = sum_(k gt.eq 0) A^(\( k \)) \, wide A^(\( k \)) in frak(A) \( B_(2^(k + 1)) \( x \) \) \, wide
   parallel A^(\( k \)) parallel lt.eq C_l parallel A parallel_(x \, l) 2^(- k l) . $ <eq:ball-decomposition>]

Conversely such a decomposition implies the approximation bound, with any slightly smaller exponent.
  We use this elementary equivalence to pass between operators and interactions on balls.

An observable supported initially on a large ball needs a separate convention.
  A family has #emph[core radius $r$ and amplitude $a$] if

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.13)")
 $ parallel A parallel lt.eq C a \, wide inf_(A_R in frak(A) \( B_R \( x \) \)) parallel A - A_R parallel lt.eq
   C_D a \( 1 + R \)^(- D) quad \( R gt.eq C_(*) r \) \, $ <eq:local-core-tail>]

where the geometric constant $C_(*)$ is independent of $D$. The amplitude may contain a specified power of $r$.
  This convention does not assert that every centered norm (3.10) is bounded by $C a$ without a further radius factor.

#thm([Lemma 3.3.])[
  (Shells outside a fixed core).  #emph[If (3.13) holds with $r gt.eq 1$, there is a decomposition $A =
    sum_(nu gt.eq 0) A_nu$ with $A_nu in frak(A) \( B_(C_(*) 2^(nu + 1) r) \( x \) \)$ and $parallel A_nu parallel
    lt.eq C_D a 2^(- nu D)$ for every $D$. Consequently, for each fixed $B gt.eq 0$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.14)")
 $ sum_(nu gt.eq 0) \( C_(*) 2^(nu + 1) r \)^B parallel A_nu parallel lt.eq C_B a r^B . $ <eq:local-core-moment>]

]
] <locality:core-shells>

#proof[
  Take approximants at radii $C_(*) 2^nu r$ and telescope, starting with the first approximant as the core.
    The core norm is $C a$; the other differences are bounded by the two adjoining errors in (3.13).
    Sum with any $D > B + 1$ to obtain (3.14). ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 3.4.])[
  (Products, commutators, and weighted sums).  #emph[The following bounds are uniform over the slot systems above.]
  + #emph[Products of operators rapidly local at the same center, and adjoints, preserve rapid locality.
    If $A \, B$ are centered at $x \, y$, respectively, with bounded local norms,
    then $parallel \[ A \, B \]_(upright("gr")) parallel lt.eq C_D \( 1 + d \( x \, y \) \)^(- D)$ for every $D$.]
  + #emph[If $A$ is rapidly local at $x$ and a rapidly local interaction $K_y$ has tempered size $a \( y \)$,
    then $\[ integral K_y thin d y \, A \]_(upright("gr"))$ is rapidly local at $x$,
    of size $a \( x \)$ times the local bounds of $A$.]
  + #emph[Suppose $A$ is even, $parallel A parallel_(x \, l) lt.eq 1$, and $J_y$ has bounded rapid local norms.
    With $D = 1 + d \( x \, y \)$ and $C_(x y) = \[ A \, J_y \]$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.15)")
 $ parallel C_(x y) parallel lt.eq C D^(- l) \, wide parallel C_(x y) parallel_(x \, l) lt.eq C \, wide
   parallel C_(x y)^(*) C_(x y) parallel_(x \, l) lt.eq C D^(- l) . $ <eq:commutator-square-locality>]

]
  #emph[For an operator initially supported in $B_r \( x \)$,
    the corresponding core estimates cost a fixed polynomial in $r$.
    At distances larger than a fixed multiple of $r$, any prescribed decay order is available.]
] <locality:calculus>

#proof[
  The product estimate follows from
  $ parallel A B - A_r B_r parallel lt.eq parallel A - A_r parallel parallel B parallel +
    parallel A_r parallel parallel B - B_r parallel . $
  For the commutator use approximations of radius less than $d \( x \, y \) \/ 3$;
    their graded commutator vanishes. For the weighted assertion expand both factors as in (3.12).
    Terms with disjoint balls vanish, while intersecting balls of radii $R \, S$ require $d \( x \, y \) lt.eq R +
    S$. The number or measure of their possible centers is $O \( \( 1 + R + S \)^2 \)$,
    and (3.11) costs at most $C \( 1 + R + S \)^kappa$. To obtain an output moment of order $D$,
    bound these costs by $C \( 1 + R + S \)^(D + kappa + 2)$ and choose input decay order larger than $D + kappa +
    6$. The two geometric sums then converge. This argument also permits assigning each output term to the center
    of either input; that convention will be used for differences of interactions.
  For (3.15), the first estimate follows by the disjoint-ball argument at exponent $l$.
    For approximation radii $r lt.eq 3 D$, use zero as an approximant to $C_(x y)$,
    giving $r^l parallel C_(x y) parallel lt.eq C$. For $r > 3 D$,
    approximate $A$ in $B_(r \/ 3) \( x \)$ and $J_y$ in $B_(r \/ 3) \( y \)$;
    their commutator is supported in $B_r \( x \)$ and has error $C r^(- l)$. Thus the second estimate holds.
    Apply the product estimate with the small norm $parallel C_(x y) parallel lt.eq
    C D^(- l)$ on one factor to obtain the third estimate. Finally a ball $B_r \( x \)$ contains
    $O \( \( 1 + r \)^2 \)$ slots, and tempered ratios within it cost $O \( \( 1 + r \)^kappa \)$.
    The same sums give the stated polynomial core costs. For tails beyond $C r$,
    the distance from the original ball is comparable to the tail radius,
    so increasing a tail order does not increase the core-volume exponent. ◻
]

#thm([Corollary 3.5.])[
  (The physical interactions).  #emph[ The unperturbed Hamiltonian $H_q$ and the Fock lift of any bounded real
    scalar symbol $phi$ have physical, neutral, Hermitian rapid interactions of uniform sizes $C_D$ and $C_D
    parallel phi parallel_oo$, respectively.]
] <locality:basic-interactions>

#proof[
  Coefficient localization gives operator-norm localization directly.
    If $E_R$ is the one-particle projection onto slots in $B_R \( x \)$,
    the CAR identity $parallel c \( f \) parallel = parallel f parallel_2$ gives
  $ parallel c \( sans(S)_q f \) - c \( E_R sans(S)_q f \) parallel = parallel \( I - E_R \) sans(S)_q f parallel_2 . $
  For $f = k_x$ or a bounded polar mode at $x$, Lemma 4 and the slot count (3.6) make the right side at most
    $C_D \( 1 + R \)^(- D)$ for every $D$; the approximating annihilator is supported in $B_R \( x \)$.
  The coherent resolution gives
  $ d Gamma \( T_q \( phi \) \) = frac(q + 1, 2 pi q) integral_(S_q) phi \( x \) c^(*) \( k_x \) c \( k_x \) thin
    d A \( x \) . $
  For $H_q$, the highest pair vector is $v_0 = e_0 and e_1$.
    Schur’s Lemma on the spin-$\( q - 1 \)$ pair space and its dimension $2 q - 1$ give, with Haar mass one,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.16)")
   $ H_q = \( 2 q - 1 \) integral_(S U \( 2 \)) B \( g v_0 \)^(*) B \( g v_0 \) thin d g .
     $ <eq:coherent-pair-resolution>]
  The product $B \( g v_0 \) = c \( g e_1 \) c \( g e_0 \)$ is rapidly local at the rotated pole by Lemmas 4
    and 7. Its phase along the stabilizer disappears in its square,
    so the center measure on the sphere has density $\( 2 q - 1 \) \/ \( 2 pi q \)$.
    This and $\( q + 1 \) \/ \( 2 pi q \)$ are uniformly bounded.
    The same two lemmas localize $c^(*) \( k_x \) c \( k_x \)$.
    No derivative of $phi$ enters either the representation or its bound. ◻
]

=== 3.3 Propagation and spectral filters <propagation-and-spectral-filters>

We next establish that a time filter preserves this local calculus.
  The relevant propagation bound is needed only with arbitrary polynomial decay,
  which permits a direct truncation argument. The underlying commutator iteration is the Lieb–Robinson method [8],
  in its graded fermionic form [13]; its use with time filters is the locality mechanism behind quasi-adiabatic
  continuation and spectral flow [6,2].

#thm([Lemma 3.6.])[
  (Propagation of local observables).  #emph[Let $K$ be self-adjoint and even,
    with a rapidly local interaction of uniform size. Write $tau_t^K \( A \) = e^(i t K) A e^(- i t K)$.
    For each $D$ there are $D'$ and $M_D$, independent of volume, such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.17)")
 $ parallel tau_t^K \( A \) parallel_(x \, D) lt.eq C_D \( 1 + \| t \| \)^(M_D) parallel A parallel_(x \, D') .
   $ <eq:polynomial-propagation>]

 The assertion holds also for time-dependent generators with the same uniform bounds.
   In particular evolution over a bounded parameter interval preserves rapid locality.
   If $A$ is supported in $B_r \( x \)$, the error in approximating $tau_t^K \( A \)$ on $B_R \( x \)$,
   for $R gt.eq C \( r + \( 1 + \| t \| \)^4 \)$, is bounded by $C_D parallel A parallel \( 1 + r \)^2 R^(- D)$.]
] <locality:dynamics>

#proof[
  Use (3.12) to write $K$ as a sum of terms on balls. Taking Hermitian and even parts makes each term Hermitian
    and even without changing the total or its estimates. First retain only terms of diameter at most $ell$.
    For this truncated interaction define
  $ M_(a b) = 2 sum_(X in.rev a \, b) parallel K_X parallel . $
  Uniform summability, including the number of sites in $X$,
    gives $sup_a sum_b M_(a b) e^(d \( a \, b \) \/ ell) lt.eq C$; indeed each retained distance is at most $ell$,
    and the ball-volume costs are polynomial. The iterated commutator inequality,
    with internal evolution removed unitarily at each step, therefore gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.18)")
   $ parallel \[ tau_t^(K_(lt.eq ell)) \( A \) \, B \]_(upright("gr")) parallel lt.eq
     C parallel A parallel parallel B parallel thin \| X \| thin \| Y \| e^(C \| t \| - d \( X \, Y \) \/ ell)
     $ <eq:truncated-LR>]
  for $A in frak(A) \( X \)$ and $B in frak(A) \( Y \)$. For completeness,
    the $n$th iterated integral contributes $\| t \|^n \/ n !$ times a chain of $n$ matrix factors $M$.
    Multiplication by $e^(d \( X \, Y \) \/ ell)$ is bounded by the product of the exponential weights along the
    chain. Summing the intermediate sites gives $C^n$, and summing $n$ gives (3.18).
    Evenness of the generator makes the same calculation valid for odd $A \, B$ with the graded commutator.
  Restore the removed terms using Duhamel’s formula. For each removed ball term use the smaller of the trivial
    commutator bound and (3.18). The centers within distance $C ell \( 1 + \| t \| \)$ of $X$,
    together with the radius of that term, occupy at most a polynomial volume.
    Outside that region the exponential bound is summable. Consequently, for every $s$, the restoration error is at most
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.19)")
   $ C_s parallel A parallel \| X \| \( 1 + \| t \| \) \( 1 + r + ell \( 1 + \| t \| \) \)^4 ell^(- s) \,
     $ <eq:range-restoration>]
  when $X subset B_r \( x \)$. To see the power allowance explicitly,
    a removed term of radius $a$ contributes its norm times at most $C \( 1 + r + ell \( 1 + \| t \| \) + a \)^2$
    possible centers and at most $C \( 1 + a \)^2$ sites. Its arbitrarily high radius moment makes the sum over
    $a > ell \/ 2$ bounded by the right side of (3.19), after increasing the input moment by $s + 6$.
  Now let $R gt.eq C \( r + \( 1 + \| t \| \)^4 \)$ and choose $ell = R^(1 \/ 3)$.
    The truncated evolution differs from the evolution generated by its terms contained in $B_R \( x \)$ by $C
    parallel A parallel \| X \| "poly" \( R \) e^(C \| t \| - c R^(2 \/ 3))$.
    This is again Duhamel’s formula and (3.18), summed over the boundary terms.
    Both restoration errors are bounded by $C_D parallel A parallel \| X \| R^(- D)$,
    choosing $s$ sufficiently large in (3.19). This supplies an approximation in $frak(A) \( B_R \( x \) \)$.
    At smaller radii use the norm bound $parallel tau_t \( A \) parallel = parallel A parallel$.
    Since $\| X \| lt.eq C \( 1 + r \)^2$, this proves the assertion for supported $A$ and then, by (3.12),
    (3.17). The proof uses only uniform bounds on the interaction at each time,
    so also proves the time-dependent assertion. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 3.7.])[
  (Time filters).  #emph[Suppose $F in L^1 \( bb(R) \)$ and $integral \( 1 + \| t \| \)^j \| F \( t \) \| thin d
    t < oo$ for every $j$. Under the hypotheses of Lemma 9,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.20)")
 $ cal(T)_(F \, K) \( A \) = integral_(bb(R)) F \( t \) tau_t^K \( A \) thin d t $ <eq:time-filter>]

 is rapidly local whenever $A$ is, with uniform bounds. The same is true for a finite measure consisting of such a
   function and a mass at zero. Number grade and physicality are preserved when $K$ is neutral and physical.
   Suitable symmetry of $F$ preserves adjoints.]
] <locality:filters>

#proof[
  Integrate (3.17). The approximation norm is a seminorm up to its operator-norm summand,
    so the integral of local approximants gives the required estimate.
    The remaining assertions follow directly by commuting the evolution with gauge transformations,
    the physical algebra, and adjoints. ◻
]

The frequency functions used below are smooth, vanish on an interval about zero,
  and have tails proportional to $1 \/ omega$ on one or both half-lines.
  Their inverse Fourier transforms satisfy the hypotheses of Lemma 10. Here is a convenient verification.
  Every positive-order derivative of such a multiplier is integrable,
  so integration by parts gives arbitrary powers of decay for $\| t \| gt.eq 1$.
  For $0 < \| t \| < 1$, split the frequency integral at $\| omega \| = \| t \|^(- 1)$.
  The inner portion is $O \( 1 + \| log \| t \| \| \)$, and one integration by parts on the outer portion is
  $O \( 1 \)$. Thus the possible singularity at zero is integrable.
  A smooth compactly supported multiplier has a Schwartz kernel.
  These facts cover both spectral transport and the filters that retain only small energy differences.

#thm([Lemma 3.8.])[
  (Weighted differences).  #emph[Let $K \, K'$ satisfy Lemma 9 uniformly, and assume $K -
    K'$ has a rapidly local interaction of tempered size $a \( z \)$. For a rapidly local $A$ centered at $x$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.21)")
 $ parallel tau_t^K \( A \) - tau_t^(K') \( A \) parallel_(x \, D) lt.eq
   C_D a \( x \) \( 1 + \| t \| \)^(M_D) parallel A parallel_(x \, D') . $ <eq:weighted-evolution-difference>]

 The same conclusion holds after an all-moments time filter, and for differences of bounded-parameter evolutions.
   If the input itself changes by local size $b \( x \)$, its separate contribution is $C b \( x \)$.
   All estimates retain the product of a local input size and $a \( x \)$.
   If $A in frak(A) \( B_r \( x \) \)$, put $s = kappa + 2$, with $kappa$ from (3.11).
   The difference has operator norm at most
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.22)")
 $ C a \( x \) parallel A parallel \( 1 + r \)^s \| t \| \( 1 + \| t \| \)^(4 s) . $ <eq:difference-core>]

 For $R gt.eq C_(*) \( 1 + r \)$, with a fixed geometric constant $C_(*)$,
   it has an approximation supported in $B_(r + R) \( x \)$ with error at most
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.23)")
 $ C_D a \( x \) parallel A parallel \( 1 + r \)^s \( 1 + \| t \| \)^(4 D + 4 s + 1) \( 1 + R \)^(- D) .
   $ <eq:difference-tail>]

 Thus the power of the initial radius is independent of the tail order.
   If the differing interaction vanishes throughout a ball of radius $d$ about $x$,
   the bounds for a fixed rapidly local input gain arbitrary inverse powers of $1 + d$.]
] <locality:difference>

#proof[
  Use the exact formula
  $ tau_t^K \( A \) - tau_t^(K') \( A \) = i integral_0^t tau_s^K \( \[ K - K' \, tau_(t - s)^(K') \( A \) \] \)
    thin d s . $
  Apply Lemma 9 to the inside observable, Lemma 7(ii) to the commutator,
    and Lemma 9 once more to the outside evolution. The tempered inequality (3.11) is precisely what allows the
    insertion at $z$ to be charged to $a \( x \)$. All time costs are polynomial and therefore integrable against
    the specified filters. The time-dependent version follows from its identical Duhamel formula.
    Linearity handles a changed input.
  We give the additional radius bookkeeping for (3.22)–(3.23).
    For a ball decomposition about $x$, use the weighted sum $sum_R \( 1 + R \)^p parallel A_R parallel$.
    Lemma 9 bounds its evolved value by $C_p \( 1 + \| t \| \)^(4 p)$ times its initial value.
    Indeed, for a radius-$r$ input, begin a dyadic decomposition at $Q = C \( 1 + r + \( 1 + \| t \| \)^4 \)$.
    The core contributes $C parallel A parallel Q^p$; the subsequent shells contribute at most $C_M parallel A
    parallel \( 1 + r \)^2 sum_(R gt.eq Q) R^(p - M)$. Choosing $M > p +
    2$ bounds this by $C_p parallel A parallel Q^p$. The weighted insertion estimate in the proof of Lemma 7 costs
    $s = kappa + 2$ powers of the input radius. Duhamel at $p = 0$ proves (3.22).
  For the tail first take $\| t \| lt.eq c R^(1 \/ 4)$ in the Duhamel integrand,
    with $c > 0$ a sufficiently small fixed constant; bounded $R$ are covered by (3.22).
    Fix also a small $epsilon > 0$. Choose $epsilon \, c$ and then $C_(*)$ so that the propagation lemma applies
    both from radius $r$ to $r + epsilon R$ and from radius $r + 3 epsilon R$ to $r + R$.
    This requires only fixed constants: if $C$ is the constant in that lemma, take $epsilon < \( 100 C \)^(- 1)$,
    $c^4 < epsilon \/ \( 10 C \)$, and $C_(*) > 100 C \/ epsilon$.
    Cut the inner evolved observable at radius $r + epsilon R$.
    Its remaining shells have weighted sum of order $s$ at most $C_M parallel A parallel \( 1 + r \)^2 R^(s - M)$,
    by the short-time bound in Lemma 9; their commutator with the insertion has the same bound times $C
    a \( x \)$. In the retained part, discard insertion terms of radius greater than $epsilon R$.
    Their arbitrary radius moments give an error $C_M a \( x \) parallel A parallel R^(- M)$.
    The remaining commutator is supported in $B_(r + 3 epsilon R) \( x \)$ and has norm at most $C a \( x \)
    parallel A parallel R^s$. Evolving it in the outside factor of Duhamel and using the short-time propagation
    estimate gives an approximation on $B_(r + R) \( x \)$ with error $C_M a \( x \) parallel A parallel
    R^(s + 2 - M)$. Choose $M$ in each of these three bounds larger than the requested order plus $s + 4$,
    and integrate over an interval of length at most $R^(1 \/ 4)$.
    This gives the stated tail bound for short times. For $\| t \| > c R^(1 \/ 4)$,
    use (3.22) and $\( 1 + \| t \| \)^(4 D) \( 1 + R \)^(- D) gt.eq c_D$.
  Finally suppose an insertion ball of radius $a$ can occur only if its center $z$ satisfies $d \( x \, z \) +
    a gt.eq d$. If that ball meets an input shell of radius $R$, then $d lt.eq R + 2 a$.
    Inserting $\( 1 + d \)^(- M) \( 1 + R + 2 a \)^M$ into the shell summation proves the last assertion,
    after increasing the input moments. This includes changes supported outside a common comparison region. ◻
]

To specify physical compression, let $S : U_q arrow.r K_S$ and $T : U_q arrow.r
  K_T$ be two slot analysis isometries. Write $J_S = Gamma \( S \)$ for the vacuum-complement embedding and
  $iota_T$ for the embedding of the physical CAR algebra into the output slots,
  acting as the identity on complementary modes. The map that we estimate is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.24)")
 $ cal(C)_(T arrow.l S) \( A \) = iota_T \( J_S^(*) A J_S \) . $ <eq:physical-compression-map>]

When $T = S$ this is compression followed by identity extension;
  it is a unital contractive completely positive map. On physical input operators it is simply change of frame.

#thm([Lemma 3.9.])[
  (Compression, changes of frame, and smooth functions).  #emph[The map (3.24), for the frames of Lemma 4,
    preserves rapid locality. An operator supported in $B_r \( x \)$ remains localized about that ball,
    with polynomial core costs and arbitrary inverse-power tails outside $B_(C r) \( x \)$.
    If $A = A^(*)$ is bounded and rapidly local, and $f$ is smooth on a neighborhood of its spectrum,
    then $f \( A \)$ is rapidly local. The constants depend on finitely many local bounds and derivatives for each
    requested decay order.]
] <locality:functional-calculus>

#proof[
  Put two slot spaces side by side. If $S \, T$ are their analysis isometries,
    the partial isometry $T S^(*)$ has rapidly decaying matrix entries by Lemma 4.
    On their direct sum, the anti-self-adjoint one-particle operator
  $ mat(delim: "(", 0, - S T^(*); T S^(*), 0) $
  rotates the two physical subspaces into each other through angle $pi \/
    2$ and acts as zero on their complements. Its second quantization is a bounded-strength,
    rapidly local quadratic interaction: absolute row sums and every distance moment are bounded by (3.6).
    Lemma 9 applies over this fixed angle. Conjugate $A ⊗ I$ by this rotation and take the vacuum expectation in
    the entire first slot copy, retaining the entire second copy.
    On a CAR monomial, the physical components have moved from $S U_q$ to $T U_q$,
    while the complementary components in the first copy are evaluated in their vacuum.
    The resulting map is exactly (3.24), including for odd monomials;
    linearity gives the identity for every operator. Vacuum expectation is contractive and preserves support in
    the doubled slot geometry. This proves the assertion, and $S = T$ gives re-embedded physical compression.
    In particular no projection onto the output complementary vacuum is left in the resulting operator.
  For the functional-calculus assertion extend $f$ smoothly to a compactly supported function on $bb(R)$.
    If $A_r$ approximates $A$ locally, its Hermitian part does so with the same error.
    The Fourier formula and Duhamel’s identity give
  $ parallel f \( A \) - f \( A_r \) parallel lt.eq (integral_(bb(R)) \| t \| thin \| hat(f) \( t \) \| thin d t)
    parallel A - A_r parallel . $
  Since $f \( A_r \)$ belongs to the same local algebra, the approximation bounds follow.
    A constant value of $f$ contributes only a scalar identity, which is local in every ball. ◻
]

=== 3.4 Pair rows and diagonal symbols <pair-rows-and-diagonal-symbols>

The coherent orbit representations already prove locality of the unperturbed Hamiltonian and bounded scalar
  perturbations. Pinning zeros will deform the pair coefficients and remove full rotation invariance.
  To control those deformations and compare neighboring fluxes,
  we now prove a local calculus directly for angular-momentum rows and diagonal orbital symbols.

Write $Q_b = B_(b - 1)$ for the normalized pair annihilator with index sum $b$ in (2.5), $1 lt.eq b lt.eq 2 q -
  1$. Define its ordered coefficients by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.25)")
 $ a_b \( i \) = frac(\( b - 2 i \) sqrt(binom(q, i) binom(q, b - i)), sqrt(S_b)) \, wide S_b =
   binom(2 q, b) frac(b \( 2 q - b \), 2 q - 1) . $ <eq:pair-symbol-normalization>]

In this formula the sum for $S_b$ is over ordered $i$. With a fixed choice of annihilator sign, $Q_b =
  2^(- 1 \/ 2) sum_(i + j = b) a_b \( i \) c_j c_i = sqrt(2) sum_(i < j \, thin i + j = b) a_b \( i \) c_j c_i$.
  Changing this common sign leaves every square unchanged.

#thm([Lemma 3.10.])[
  (Pair profiles and local row squares).  #emph[With $m = b \/ 2$ and $w = w_q \( m \)$,
    the zero-extended coefficients satisfy
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.26)")
 $ \| Delta_i^r Delta_b^s a_b \( i \) \| lt.eq C_(D \, r \, s) w^(- 1 \/ 2 - r - s)
   (1 + frac(\| i - m \|, w))^(- D) . $ <eq:pair-row-symbols>]

 There are pair annihilators $L_(nu \, phi.alt)$, rapidly local with uniform bounds about centers
   $x_(nu \, phi.alt)$, and a measure $d mu$ of bounded mass per unit ball, such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.27)")
 $ H_q = sum_nu integral_0^(2 pi) L_(nu \, phi.alt)^(*) L_(nu \, phi.alt) thin d mu_nu \( phi.alt \) .
   $ <eq:pair-local-resolution>]

 The construction is linear in the row coefficients. In particular symbol bounds (3.26) with an additional
   tempered factor give local bounds with that factor; the same holds for differences of row families in common frames.]
] <locality:row-symbols>

#proof[
  Vandermonde’s identity identifies $binom(q, i) binom(q, b - i) \/
    binom(2 q, b)$ as a hypergeometric law of mean $b \/ 2$ and variance
    $b \( 2 q - b \) \/ \( 4 \( 2 q - 1 \) \)$. Multiplying its second centered moment by four proves (3.25).
    The moment formula itself also follows directly by applying $i binom(q, i) =
    q binom(q - 1, i - 1)$ twice and Vandermonde’s identity.
  Here are symbol estimates that include the dependence on the moving row center. Set
  $ ell_q \( x \) = log Gamma \( q + 1 \) - log Gamma \( x + 1 \) - log Gamma \( q - x + 1 \) \, quad t = i - m \, $
  and
  $ E \( m \, t \) = 1 / 2 { ell_q \( m + t \) + ell_q \( m - t \) } - ell_q \( m \) \, wide T \( m \) =
    S_(2 m) e^(- 2 ell_q \( m \)) \, $
  where the gamma formula defines the smooth extension. Then $a_b \( i \) = -
    2 t e^(E \( m \, t \)) \/ sqrt(T \( m \))$. The gamma duplication formula gives the exact expression
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.28)")
   $ T \( m \) = frac(4 m \( q - m \), 2 q - 1) sqrt(pi) thin frac(R \( m \) R \( q - m \), R \( q \)) \, wide
     R \( x \) = frac(Gamma \( x + 1 \), Gamma \( x + 1 \/ 2 \)) . $ <eq:pair-moving-normalization>]
  For $m \, q - m gt.eq 1 \/ 2$, Stirling’s inequalities yield $T \( m \) asymp w_q \( m \)^3$.
    Its logarithmic derivatives of order $r gt.eq 1$ are bounded by $C_r \( u^(- r) + v^(- r) \)$.
    These derivative assertions can be obtained directly by differentiating Euler’s gamma product: for $k gt.eq 1$,
  $ frac(d^(k + 1), d x^(k + 1)) log Gamma \( x \) = \( - 1 \)^(k + 1) k ! sum_(n = 0)^oo \( x + n \)^(- k - 1) . $
  In the region $\| t \| lt.eq c min \( u \, v \)$, the second derivative $ell''_q$ is negative and comparable in
    magnitude to $u^(- 1) + v^(- 1) asymp w^(- 2)$. Thus $E \( m \, t \) lt.eq - c t^2 \/ w^2$.
    Differentiating $E$ in $i$ and $b$ gives polynomials in $t \/ w$, with one factor $w^(- 1)$ per derivative;
    (3.28) has the same property. The discrete product rule, or integration of these derivative bounds over unit
    cubes, proves (3.26) in this region. There is also a global Gaussian bound, including the ends:
    convexity of the trigamma function $psi_1 = \( log Gamma \)''$ and $psi_1 \( x \) gt.eq 1 \/ x$ give
  $ E_(t t) \( m \, t \) = - 1 / 2 { psi_1 \( m + t + 1 \) + psi_1 \( m - t + 1 \) + psi_1 \( q - m + t + 1 \) +
    psi_1 \( q - m - t + 1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[}] lt.eq - 1 / u - 1 / v lt.eq - c / w^2 . $
  Since $E \( m \, 0 \) = E_t \( m \, 0 \) = 0$, we have $E \( m \, t \) lt.eq -
    c t^2 \/ w^2$ on the entire allowed interval. Outside the central region $\| t \| \/ w gt.eq
    c w$ when $w$ is large, so increasing the tail order pays all derivative and endpoint-extension factors.
    The rows with bounded $w$ require only bounded finite differences.
    This proves the full uniform estimate without differentiating a lattice normalization sum.
  Choose smooth windows $zeta_nu \( b \)$ with $sum_nu zeta_nu \( b \)^2 = 1$,
    supported on intervals of length $O \( W_nu \)$ around $b = 2 M_nu$, where $W_nu = w_q \( M_nu \)$.
    The construction (3.3), with $m = b \/ 2$, supplies them. Define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.29)")
   $ L_(nu \, phi.alt) = W_nu^(- 1 \/ 2) sum_b zeta_nu \( b \) e^(- i b phi.alt) Q_b \, wide
     d mu_nu \( phi.alt \) = W_nu frac(d phi.alt, 2 pi) . $ <eq:fourier-pair-localization>]
  Fourier orthogonality proves (3.27) exactly. The radial centers have bounded density in physical distance,
    while their circle lengths are comparable to $W_nu$ outside the polar balls.
    Hence $mu$ has bounded mass on unit balls.
  After removing its linear phase $e^(- i \( i + j \) phi.alt)$,
    the two-index coefficient of $L_(nu \, phi.alt)$ has height $O \( W_nu^(- 1) \)$,
    and every mixed finite difference of orders $r \, s$ costs $W_nu^(- r - s)$.
    Its tails are arbitrary powers of $1 + \| i - M_nu \| \/ W_nu + \| j - M_nu \| \/ W_nu$.
    To express it in slot fermions, multiply in each index by the window coefficients from (3.4) and sum.
    On comparable windows, repeated summation by parts in both indices gives, for arbitrary $D$, coefficients bounded by
  $ C_D \( 1 + d \( x \, x_(nu \, phi.alt) \) \)^(- D) \( 1 + d \( y \, x_(nu \, phi.alt) \) \)^(- D) $
  for the slot monomial $c_x c_y$. The height is bounded because the two summation lengths are $O \( W_nu \)$,
    the coefficient height is $O \( W_nu^(- 1) \)$, and the two Fourier normalizations contribute
    $O \( W_nu^(- 1) \)$. Noncomparable windows have radial separation;
    their scale ratios cost polynomial powers by (3.2), paid by increasing the radial tail orders.
    This proves the displayed bound for all slots. Its absolute sum, including every distance moment,
    is bounded by (3.6). Since $parallel c_x c_y parallel lt.eq 1$,
    truncating that sum proves rapid locality in operator norm.
    Products give locality of $L_(nu \, phi.alt)^(*) L_(nu \, phi.alt)$.
    Each operation used was linear in the row coefficients before the final square;
    keeping any extra tempered factor proves the last assertion. ◻
]

For a smooth real function $a$ on $\[ 0 \, q \]$, fix a constant $C_0$ larger than the window-support constants
  and define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.30)")
 $ frak(s)_K \( a \; m \) = max_(0 lt.eq k lt.eq K) sup_(j in \[ 0 \, q \]\
\| j - m \| lt.eq C_0 w_q \( m \)) w_q \( m \)^k \| a^(\( k \)) \( j \) \| . $ <eq:diagonal-symbol-seminorm>]

The order-zero term is part of this seminorm. Scaled derivatives, rather than merely bounded unscaled derivatives,
  are necessary: orbital oscillations on the scale of one index translate an operator around a macroscopic latitude.

#thm([Lemma 3.11.])[
  (Diagonal symbols).  #emph[Suppose $A \( m \) > 0$ is tempered with exponent $kappa$ and $frak(s)_K \( a \; m \)
    lt.eq C_K A \( m \)$ for every integer $K gt.eq 0$. Then $D_q \( a \) =
    sum_j a \( j \) c_j^(*) c_j$ has a physical, neutral, Hermitian interaction of size $A \( m \)$.
    For each output approximation order $D$, only the seminorms through $K_D = ceil.l 4 D + 4 kappa +
    40 ceil.r$ are needed. The construction and the estimate are linear in the symbol,
    so the same assertion holds for differences of symbols.]
] <locality:one-body-symbol>

#proof[
  Insert the analysis map on both sides of the diagonal one-particle matrix. The slot matrix elements are
  $ \( L_(a_1) L_(a_2) \)^(- 1 \/ 2) sum_j a \( j \) xi_(a_1) \( j \) xi_(a_2) \( j \)
    e^(i j \( phi.alt_2 - phi.alt_1 \)) . $
  The two radial windows must overlap, so their widths are comparable and their radial physical separation is
    bounded. Taking $K$ finite differences of the summand and summing by parts gives the bound $C_K
    A \( m_1 \) \( 1 + d \( x_1 \, x_2 \) \)^(- K)$. The normalization cancels the length of the summation
    interval. At bounded-width end windows the same statement follows from finite sums;
    all involved slots are inside a bounded polar ball.
  Assign the quadratic monomial to $x_1$, and pair it with its adjoint to obtain Hermitian terms.
    Vacuum-compress each assigned term using (3.24). Compression fixes the physical total $D_q \( a \)$,
    so the resulting decomposition is still exact and now each term is physical.
    A monomial joining points at distance $R$ is supported in $B_(1 + R) \( x_1 \)$;
    the compression lemma bounds its approximation norm of order $D$ by $C_D \( 1 + R \)^(D + 2)$.
    The slot count (3.6), the tempered ratios, and $K > D + kappa +
    6$ therefore make the assigned absolute sum converge with the required $D$th moment.
    The stated value of $K_D$ more than covers these losses and the fixed finite differences of the windows.
    The grade and reality are preserved by compression. Every step is linear before pairing adjoints,
    which also proves the difference assertion. ◻
]

We record explicitly how the symbol criterion applies after subtracting an affine function.
  Let $rho \( j \, m \)$ denote meridional distance between the latitudes of indices $j \, m$,
  and fix a center $x$ at index $m$. Suppose there are $beta > 0$, $kappa gt.eq 0$, and constants $C_k$ such that,
  for every $k gt.eq 2$ and $j in \[ 0 \, q \]$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.31)")
 $ w_q \( j \)^k \| a^(\( k \)) \( j \) \| lt.eq C_k beta \( 1 + rho \( j \, m \) \)^kappa .
   $ <eq:affine-symbol-hypothesis>]

Define $tilde(a)_x \( j \) = a \( j \) - a \( m \) - a' \( m \) \( j - m \)$. Taylor’s formula and (3.2) imply

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.32)")
 $ frak(s)_K \( tilde(a)_x \; m_y \) lt.eq C_K beta \( 1 + d \( x \, y \) \)^(kappa + 8) . $ <eq:affine-symbol-size>]

Here is the bound for the two orders not already present in (3.31).
  The index excursion between $m$ and $j$ is at most a constant times $w_q \( j \) \( 1 + rho \( j \, m \) \)^2$,
  and the ratio of $w_q \( j \)$ to the minimum of $w_q$ on that meridian segment is polynomially bounded by
  $\( 1 + rho \( j \, m \) \)^2$. Integrating $a''$ once or twice therefore bounds $w_q \( j \) \|
  tilde(a)'_x \( j \) \|$ and $\| tilde(a)_x \( j \) \|$ by the right side of (3.32).
  On each window the widths are comparable and meridional distance changes by a bounded amount,
  proving the displayed seminorm bound.

Lemma 14 and the weighted commutator estimate now show that $\[ D_q \( tilde(a)_x \) \, O_x \]$ has rapid local
  size $C beta$ whenever $O_x$ has bounded rapid local norms at $x$.
  In applications one can take $beta$ comparable to $w_q \( m \)^2 \| a'' \( m \) \|$ and verify (3.31) directly.
  For an annular sum commuting with $cal(N)$ and $sum_j j c_j^(*) c_j$,
  replacing $a$ by $tilde(a)_x$ leaves the commutator unchanged:
  the removed operator is a linear combination of those two conserved quantities.

Commutators, evolution, and spectral functions of physical operators remain physical,
  and neutral evolution preserves number grade. The strictly supported approximants used to measure locality need
  not preserve the vacant-complement subspace; physical vacuum compression is available when a physical
  representative is required. The frame may therefore be fixed while proving an operator estimate,
  changed when rotating an observable, or chosen compatibly at nearby fluxes.
  Lemmas 10 and 11 ensure that the same local estimates remain available after the spectral transports used next.

== 4 Pinning zeros and transporting weighted observables <sec:pinning>

We compare zero modes at fluxes differing by one or three.
  Multiplication by a zero at the south pole gives the algebraic comparison, but is not unitary.
  We construct a unitary comparison whose effect on a local observable is small away from that pole.
  For a convex orbital weight we also retain a sign in this comparison.
  That sign will be used in the first charge estimate in Section 6.

Fix $d in { 1 \, 3 }$ and put $t = q - d$. The identification of orbital labels $0 \, dots.h \,
  t$ embeds $U_t$ isometrically into $U_q$; write $iota_(q \, d)$ for its exterior-algebra extension.
  Its image has the last $d$ orbitals empty. Set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ g_(q \, d) \( j \) = 1 / 2 log binom(t, j) / binom(q, j) = 1 /
   2 sum_(r = 0)^(d - 1) log frac(q - j - r, q - r) \, wide 0 lt.eq j lt.eq t . $ <eq:pinning-log-weight>]

Multiplication of a polynomial by the $d$ south-pole zero factors, one factor for each particle,
  is $iota_(q \, d) exp D_t \( g_(q \, d) \)$, up to a scalar on each number sector.
  In particular, the subspace of $Z_(n \, q)$ having these zeros is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
 $ iota_(q \, d) exp D_t \( g_(q \, d) \) Z_(n \, t) . $ <eq:pinning-kernel-identification>]

Throughout this section, $m$, $u = 1 + m$, $v = 1 + q - m$,
  and $w = \( u v \/ \( q + 1 \) \)^(1 \/ 2)$ use the larger flux $q$.
  Replacing $q$ by $t$ in these size functions changes them by bounded factors.
  For every integer $ell gt.eq 1$, the smooth extension of Equation (4.1) satisfies, for $0 lt.eq m lt.eq t$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
 $ g_(q \, d)^(\( ell \)) \( m \) = - frac(\( ell - 1 \) !, 2) sum_(r = 0)^(d - 1) \( q - m - r \)^(- ell) \,
   wide \| g_(q \, d)^(\( ell \)) \( m \) \| lt.eq C_ell v^(- ell) \, wide -
   g''_(q \, d) \( m \) asymp v^(- 2) . $ <eq:pinning-derivatives>]

Here and below constants can depend on $d$ and on a stated differentiation or localization order, but not on $q$,
  $n$, or $m$.

=== 4.1 A uniformly gapped path <a-uniformly-gapped-path>

For $1 lt.eq b lt.eq 2 t - 1$, let $B_(t \, b)$ be the normalized pair annihilator whose coefficient at $i < j$,
  $i + j = b$, is proportional to $\( j - i \) sqrt(binom(t, i) binom(t, j))$. For $0 lt.eq s lt.eq 1$ define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
 $ B_(q \, d \, b) \( s \) & = sum_(0 lt.eq i < j lt.eq t\
i + j = b) a_(q \, d \, b \, s) \( i \, j \) c_j c_i \,\
a_(q \, d \, b \, s) \( i \, j \) & =
  frac(a_(t \, b) \( i \, j \) e^(- s \( g_(q \, d) \( i \) + g_(q \, d) \( j \) \)), (sum_(k < l \, med k + l =
  b) \| a_(t \, b) \( k \, l \) \|^2 e^(- 2 s \( g_(q \, d) \( k \) + g_(q \, d) \( l \) \)))^(1 \/ 2)) .
  $ <eq:pinning-rows>]

and

$ H_(q \, d) \( s \) = sum_b B_(q \, d \, b) \( s \)^(*) B_(q \, d \, b) \( s \) \, wide P_(q \, d) \( s \) =
  upright("the orthogonal projection onto ") ker H_(q \, d) \( s \) . $

All these operators act on $cal(F)_t$. Direct conjugation of each annihilator gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.5)")
 $ ker H_(q \, d) \( s \) = e^(s D_t \( g_(q \, d) \)) ker H_t . $ <eq:pinning-path-kernel>]

The normalization in Equation (4.4) only multiplies each row by a positive scalar and therefore does not affect
  this identity.

#thm([Lemma 4.1.])[
  (Gap along the pinning path).  #emph[There exist $q_(upright("pin"))$ and $gamma_(upright("pin")) > 0$ such that
$ H_(q \, d) \( s \) gt.eq gamma_(upright("pin")) \( 1 - P_(q \, d) \( s \) \)
  quad \( q gt.eq q_(upright("pin")) \, med d in { 1 \, 3 } \, med 0 lt.eq s lt.eq 1 \) $

 on the entire Fock space $cal(F)_(q - d)$.]
] <pinning:uniform-gap>

