=== 4.2 Local transport and comparison of consecutive fluxes <local-transport-and-comparison-of-consecutive-fluxes>

For the remainder of this section all compared path fluxes are assumed to be at least $q_(upright("pin"))$.
  Taking $q gt.eq q_(upright("pin")) + 6$ ensures this for the comparisons of size at most three and the growth
  steps used below. Larger fixed comparison ranges require only a corresponding fixed increase in this margin.

We compare different orbital dimensions by a fixed convention.
  If $t lt.eq t'$, identify orbitals with the same labels and extend an even operator $A$ on $cal(F)_t$ to $A ⊗
  I$ on $cal(F)_(t')$, using the graded tensor decomposition over the additional modes.
  Its original matrix elements are recovered by putting those modes in the vacuum.
  We then use the common slot frames of Lemma 5. All operator differences below use this padding convention.

The local rate of the transport will be

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.8)")
 $ alpha_q \( m \) = w^2 / v^2 . $ <eq:pinning-alpha>]

In the north this rate is of order $\( 1 + m \) \/ q^2$; changing the flux by a bounded amount will gain one more
  inverse power of $v$.

#thm([Proposition 4.2.])[
  (Transport of the pinned spaces).  #emph[There is a number-preserving and axially invariant unitary path
    $U_(q \, d) \( s \)$ on $cal(F)_t$, starting at the identity, such that
$ U_(q \, d) \( s \) ker H_t = ker H_(q \, d) \( s \) . $

 Writing $partial_s U_(q \, d) \( s \) = Y_(q \, d) \( s \) U_(q \, d) \( s \)$,
   the anti-Hermitian generator $Y_(q \, d) \( s \)$ is a rapid interaction of size $C alpha_q \( m \)$.
   In common frames, a bounded change in $q$, with $d$ fixed,
   changes the generator interaction by size $C alpha_q \( m \) \/ v$.]
] <pinning:generator>

