The remaining issue in this decomposition is analytic. A polar decomposition need not be local when its nonzero
  singular values approach zero. The next lemma rules out that problem uniformly in the flux.

=== 5.2 A local polar map <a-local-polar-map>

#thm([Lemma 5.2.])[
  (Occupation gap and local representatives).  #emph[There is $epsilon > 0$,
    independent of $q$ and of the number sector, such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.4)")
 $ P_q n_q P_q gt.eq epsilon P_(cal(E)_q) \, wide cal(E)_q = xor.big_n cal(E)_(n \, q) . $ <eq:flags-occupation-gap>]

 The polar map $sans(T)_q$ and the projections onto the two branches, restricted to $Z_q$,
   have bounded physical representatives rapidly localized at the south pole.
   The representatives preserve $Z_q$ and its orthogonal complement.
   They have respectively the number and index grades of $c_q$ and of the identity.]
] <lem:flags-polar>

#proof[
  We first prove the singular-value bound, keeping track of the restriction of pair rows. The normalized rows are
  $ B_(q \, b) = sum_(0 lt.eq i < j lt.eq q\
i + j = b) a_(q \, b) \( i \, j \) c_j c_i \, wide a_(q \, b) \( i \, j \) =
  frac(\( j - i \) sqrt(binom(q, i) binom(q, j)), sqrt(q binom(2 q - 2, b - 1))) \, quad 1 lt.eq b lt.eq 2 q - 1 . $
  For completeness, their normalization follows by taking coefficients in
  $ sum_(i < j) \( j - i \)^2 binom(q, i) binom(q, j)
    x^(i + j) = \( 1 + x \)^q \( x partial_x \)^2 \( 1 + x \)^q -
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( x partial_x \) \( 1 + x \)^q #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^2 =
    q x \( 1 + x \)^(2 q - 2) . $
  If a row contains orbital $q$, put $k = 2 q - b$. Its removed coefficient mass is
  $ p_(q \, k) = frac(k binom(q - 1, k - 1), binom(2 q - 2, k - 1)) . $
  Here $p_(q \, 1) = p_(q \, 2) = 1$, and
  $ p_(q \, k + 1) / p_(q \, k) = frac(\( k + 1 \) \( q - k \), k \( 2 q - k - 1 \)) lt.eq 1 . $
  It follows that every row with a retained pair has retained mass
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.5)")
   $ r_(q \, b) = sum_(i < j < q\
i + j = b) \| a_(q \, b) \( i \, j \) \|^2 gt.eq frac(q, 4 q - 6) gt.eq 1 / 4 . $ <eq:flags-retained-mass>]
  Rows with no coefficient at $q$ have mass one. The two top rows retain no pair and are omitted.
  For $d = 1$, the pinning weights obey $g_j = 1 / 2 log \( \( q - j \) \/ q \)$.
    Multiplication of its coefficients by $e^(- g_i - g_j)$, followed by normalization,
    turns a flux-$\( q - 1 \)$ row into the normalized retained flux-$q$ row.
    More precisely, if $B_(q \, b)^(upright("ret"))$ denotes the row with the coefficient at orbital $q$ deleted,
    then $B_(q \, b)^(upright("ret")) = sqrt(r_(q \, b)) B_(q \, 1 \, b) \( 1 \)$,
    in the notation of Equation (4.4). Let $delta > 0$ be the uniform endpoint gap from Lemma 15.
    By (5.5), the Hamiltonian compressed to the south-empty sector has a gap at least $delta \/
    4$ above $xor.big_n cal(F)_(n \, q)$.
  The CAR give the exact identity
  $ sum_b \[ B_(q \, b) \, n_q \]^(*) \[ B_(q \, b) \, n_q \] = n_q sum_(k = 1)^q p_(q \, k) n_(q - k) . $
  The coefficient sum is
  $ sum_(k = 1)^q p_(q \, k) = frac(4 q - 2, q + 1) < 4 . $
  One way to see this is to write $p_(q \, k) = k binom(2 q - k - 1, q - 1) \/
    binom(2 q - 2, q - 1)$ and apply the hockey-stick identity twice.
    For $psi in Z_q$, the two top singleton rows also imply $c_(q - 1) c_q psi = c_(q - 2) c_q psi = 0$. Hence
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.6)")
   $ sum_b parallel \[ B_(q \, b) \, n_q \] psi parallel^2 lt.eq
     2 parallel c_q psi parallel^2 . $ <eq:flags-emptying-energy>]
  Let $Q = 1 - n_q$ and let $P_F$ project onto $xor.big_n cal(F)_(n \, q)$.
    Since $B_(q \, b) psi = 0$, the restricted gap gives
  $ delta / 4 parallel Q psi - P_F psi parallel^2 lt.eq sum_b parallel B_(q \, b) Q psi parallel^2 lt.eq
    2 parallel c_q psi parallel^2 . $
  The occupied and vacant components are orthogonal, so
  $ parallel psi - P_F psi parallel^2 lt.eq \( 1 + 8 \/ delta \) parallel c_q psi parallel^2 . $
  Thus (5.4) holds with $epsilon = delta \/ \( delta + 8 \)$.
  To construct local representatives, take a real smooth frequency cutoff $chi$ equal to one near zero and
    supported strictly inside the uniform unperturbed gap. Apply its time filter to $c_q$:
  $ A_q = integral_(bb(R)) hat(chi) \( t \) e^(i t H_q) c_q e^(- i t H_q) thin d t \, $
  with the Fourier normalization incorporated into $hat(chi)$. Contraction preserves zero modes.
    The cutoff therefore gives $A_q P_q = c_q P_q$ and $\[ A_q \, P_q \] = 0$.
    The local calculus makes $A_q$ rapidly localized at the south pole, with uniform constants.
    Choose a smooth function $eta$ on a fixed interval containing the spectrum of $A_q^(*) A_q$,
    equal to zero near zero and to $x^(- 1 \/ 2)$ for $x gt.eq epsilon$. Then
  $ tilde(sans(T))_q = A_q eta \( A_q^(*) A_q \) $
  is rapidly localized by smooth functional calculus and agrees with $sans(T)_q$ on $Z_q$.
    Its products $tilde(sans(T))_q^(*) tilde(sans(T))_q$ and $1 -
    tilde(sans(T))_q^(*) tilde(sans(T))_q$ represent the branch projections on $Z_q$.
    The construction preserves number and index grades because $H_q$ preserves both and $A_q^(*)
    A_q$ has grade zero. It also preserves $P_q$ in both directions.
    No claim about a spectral gap of $A_q^(*) A_q$ outside $Z_q$ is needed:
    $eta$ is smooth on that entire spectral interval. ◻
]

=== 5.3 One-step comparisons <one-step-comparisons>

Write

$ K_(q \, d) \( f \) = cal(U)_(q \, d)^(*) D_q \( f \) cal(U)_(q \, d) - D_(q - d) \( f \) $

on the corresponding smaller zero space. Proposition  18 supplies localized representatives for these operators,
  with the stated size and flux-comparison estimates.

#thm([Proposition 5.3.])[
  (Weighted observable at one branch).  #emph[Fix $p in \( 0 \, 2 \)$, $L gt.eq 2$, and $f \( j \) =
    f_(L \, p) \( j \)$. In the orthogonal coordinates (5.3),
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.7)")
 $ \( J_(q \, F) xor J_(q \, E) \)^(*) D_q \( f \) \( J_(q \, F) xor J_(q \, E) \) =  &
   mat(delim: "(", D_(q - 1) \( f \) + K_(q \, 1) \( f \), 0; 0, D_(q - 3) \( f \) + f \( q \) + K_(q \, 3) \(
   f \)) + R_q \( f \) \,\
 & parallel R_q \( f \) parallel lt.eq C_p frac(f \( q \), \( L + q \)^2) . $ <eq:flags-weighted-step>]

 The notation in the left side means the unitary whose columns are the two branch isometries.
   The bound includes the off-diagonal blocks. For affine $f$ the remainder is zero and $K_(q \, d) \( f \) = 0$.]
] <prop:flags-weighted-step>

#proof[
  The $F$ block is the definition of $K_(q \, 1) \( f \)$. For an affine sequence, the grades of $sans(T)_q$ give
  $ D_q \( f \) sans(T)_q = sans(T)_q D_q \( f \) - f \( q \) sans(T)_q $
  on the zero spaces, and both branch projections commute with $D_q \( f \)$.
    The endpoint transports commute with the number and index operators.
    This proves the affine assertion, including the scalar $f \( q \)$ in $E$.
  For a general $f$, subtract its affine Taylor polynomial at $q$.
    The remainder $a \( j \) = f \( j \) - f \( q \) - f' \( q \) \( j - q \)$ satisfies
  $ \| a \( j \) \| lt.eq C_p frac(f \( q \), \( L + q \)^2) \( 1 + q - j \)^(p + 4) \, wide 0 lt.eq j lt.eq q . $
  The same polynomial bounds hold for the finite differences needed for localization.
    In the south-pole frame this says that $D_q \( a \)$ has one-body interaction size at most $C_p
    f \( q \) \/ \( L + q \)^2$, times a fixed polynomial in distance from the pole.
    Commuting it with any of the rapidly localized representatives in Lemma 22 thus has norm at most that size:
    decompose the representative into ball shells and sum its arbitrarily high inverse-power tails against this
    polynomial. In the $E$ block use $sans(T)_q \[ D_q \( a \) \, sans(T)_q^(*) \]$;
    for the off-diagonal blocks use the commutator with a branch projection.
    Compression and the endpoint isometries do not increase these norms. This proves (5.7). ◻
]

For a general observable supported far to the north, the polar map contributes an even smaller error,
  because its representative is localized at the opposite pole. We record the precise form used below.

#thm([Lemma 5.4.])[
  (Northern comparison).  #emph[There are fixed $c gt.eq 0$ and $b_0 gt.eq 1$ with the following property.
    Let $O = O^(*)$ be neutral, of norm at most one, supported on a slot ball of radius $r gt.eq
    2$ centered at latitude index $m$, and put $u = 1 + m$. If $q > C r^(b_0) u$,
    identify all northern slots in the fluxes being compared. The two diagonal branch blocks of $O$ equal
$ O + cal(D)_(q \, 1) \( O \) quad upright("and") quad O + cal(D)_(q \, 3) \( O \) $

 up to a matrix remainder of norm $C_D q^(- D)$ for every fixed $D$.
   Each $cal(D)_(q \, d) \( O \)$ is neutral and self-adjoint.
   If $x$ is its original center, then, for fixed $c \, C_(*) < oo$ and every $D > 0$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.8)")
 $ parallel cal(D)_(q \, d) \( O \) parallel & lt.eq C r^c u \/ q^2 \,\
inf_(A_R in frak(A) \( B_R \( x \) \)) parallel cal(D)_(q \, d) \( O \) - A_R parallel & lt.eq
  C_D r^c u q^(- 2) R^(- D) wide \( R gt.eq C_(*) r \) . $ <eq:flags-northern-size>]

 For $\| q - q' \| lt.eq 2$ and fixed $d$, the difference between these changes, in common northern slots,
   obeys the same two estimates with $q^(- 2)$ replaced by $q^(- 3)$.
   The exponent $c$ and core constant $C_(*)$ are independent of $D$.]
] <lem:flags-northern-step>

#proof[
  For $F$, this is Corollary 17. Its norm and tail estimates are Equations (4.11) and (4.11);
    enlarge the fixed core constant to write the outer ball radius as $R$ instead of $r + R$.
    For $E$, commute $O$ through the local representative of $sans(T)_q$ before applying that corollary with $d =
    3$. The support of $O$ is a distance comparable with $sqrt(q)$ from the south pole when $b_0$ is increased if
    necessary. Rapid locality therefore makes the polar commutator and the off-branch blocks $O \( q^(- D) \)$ for
    every $D$. The same estimate handles the finitely many trimmed orbitals.
    The difference assertion for the remaining changes is the same-corollary comparison of the pinning transports
    at bounded flux difference. ◻
]

=== 5.4 Histories and a linear reference expectation <histories-and-a-linear-reference-expectation>

Iterate the two branch coordinates until the flux reaches $q_(upright("stop"))$.
  A word $omega$ records its successive $F$ and $E$ choices;
  its terminal number and flux are denoted by $n_omega$ and $q_omega$. The resulting unitary is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.9)")
 $ cal(R)_(n \, q) : Z_(n \, q) arrow.r xor.big_omega Z_(n_omega \, q_omega) . $ <eq:flags-history-map>]

For a fixed readout axis and fixed filters, the maps $J_(q \, F)$, $J_(q \, E)$,
  and $cal(R)_(n \, q)$ are defined on physical Fock spaces independently of the auxiliary slot frames.
  Rebuilding frames outside the common northern slots changes only the representations used to prove locality
  estimates; it does not redefine these maps or their history coordinates.
  An $F$ step taken at flux $b$ records one flag with value $b$.
  At a terminal space we add $q_omega + 3 - 3 n_omega$ flags, all with value zero.
  Let $cal(B) \( omega \)$ be this multiset. Each word has exactly $h$ flags.
  The vacuum follows only $F$ steps, with the same terminal convention.
  We do not resolve the bounded-dimensional terminal space further.

Fix $a in \( 0 \, 2 \)$. On the direct sum in (5.9), define the positive diagonal operator

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.10)")
 $ W_m \( omega \) = sum_(b in cal(B) \( omega \)) min {1 \, (frac(1 + m, 1 + b))^a} . $ <eq:flags-history-weight>]

Its value is scalar on each terminal summand. In particular $W_m = 0$ on the filled zero space,
  and $W_m gt.eq \( 1 + q \)^(- a)$ whenever $h > 0$.

We next fix the scalar used to center observables. Put $q^0 = 3 floor.l q \/ 3 floor.r$,
  and let $Omega_(q^0)$ be the normalized filled zero mode at that flux.
  Embed its one-particle space into $U_q$ by sending orbital $j$ to orbital $j$,
  and then use the slot embedding at flux $q$, with the orthogonal complement of the embedded one-particle space
  vacant. Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.11)")
 $ "ref"_q \( O \) = chevron.l Omega_(q^0) \, O Omega_(q^0) chevron.r $ <eq:flags-reference>]

in this padded realization. This is a positive linear functional of norm one on the slot algebra.
  At $q = q^0$ it is the filled expectation. For $q^0 = 0$ the convention is $U_0 =
  bb(C)$ with its unique normalized one-electron filled vector.
  Outside the common northern region only linearity, the norm-one bound, and the exact filled expectation at $q =
  q^0$ will be used.

#thm([Lemma 5.5.])[
  (Compatibility of reference expectations).  #emph[Let two fluxes $q \, q'$ use common northern slots,
    and let $s lt.eq min \( q \, q' \)$. Embed a fixed vector of $cal(F)_s$ into either system by the same orbital
    labels, with the orthogonal complement of the embedded one-particle space vacant.
    The two states have identical expectations on the algebra of common northern slots.
    In particular, references at fluxes with the same nearest lower filled flux agree on that algebra.]
] <lem:flags-reference-compatibility>

#proof[
  Expand a slot observable into normally ordered CAR monomials.
    Its expectation in an embedded state is a linear combination of the state’s normally ordered orbital
    correlations. The coefficients use only the analysis-frame entries of the slots occurring in the monomial.
    Entries at orbital labels above $s$ contribute zero, because those orbitals are vacant.
    At labels at most $s$, the common northern frame entries are identical.
    Thus every monomial has the same expectation in both realizations.
    This also proves the assertion after physical compression of the slot observable,
    which leaves these matrix elements unchanged. ◻
]

The parent and its $E$ child use filled states at $q^0$ and $q^0 - 3$, respectively.
  Their expectations are related by the filled $E$ step. By contrast, the references at $q - 3$ and $q^0 -
  3$ use the same filled state and agree on common northern slots by Lemma 25.
  For a rapidly localized operator, apply this exact identity to a truncation supported in those slots;
  the remaining difference is at most twice the truncation error.
  The common-frame construction permits these conventions for every frame entering a comparison, with uniform estimates.

#thm([Theorem 5.6.])[
  (Local observables in history coordinates).  #emph[For every fixed $a in \( 0 \, 2 \)$ there are $B \,
    C < oo$ such that the following holds uniformly in $n \, q$ and in the allowed slot frames.
    Let $O = O^(*)$ be a neutral slot observable of norm at most one, supported in a ball of radius $r gt.eq
    2$ with center of latitude index $m$. Its compression to $Z_(n \, q)$ satisfies
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.12)")
 $ plus.minus (cal(R)_(n \, q) P_(n \, q) O P_(n \, q) cal(R)_(n \, q)^(*) - "ref"_q \( O \) I) lt.eq
   C r^B W_m . $ <eq:flags-local-readout>]

 Here $P_(n \, q)$ denotes the zero-space projection in the number-$n$ sector.
   More generally, for a convergent decomposition $A = sum_nu
   A_nu$ into neutral self-adjoint observables supported in balls about the same center,
   of respective radii $r_nu gt.eq 2$, the right side can be replaced by $C W_m sum_nu r_nu^B parallel A_nu parallel$.]
] <thm:flags-local-readout>

#proof[
  The proof keeps $O$ unchanged while descending through common northern slots.
    Only the small changes caused by each transport are estimated inductively.
    This distinction prevents an increasing radius from being assigned repeatedly to the original observable.
  We first describe the centered one-step identities under the northern condition of Lemma 24.
    For an $E$ step put $t = q - 3$, so $t^0 = q^0 - 3$, and write $R_(q \, E) \( O \)$ for its uncentered polar
    remainder in Lemma 24. The filled $E$ step at $q^0$, together with compatibility of the northern references,
    gives the exact scalar identity
  $ "ref"_q \( O \) - "ref"_t \( O \) = "ref"_(t^0) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cal(D)_(q^0 \, 3) \( O \) +
    R_(q^0 \, E) \( O \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Thus the parent-to-child reference change is determined by a filled step. Define
  $ R'_(q \, E) \( O \) & = R_(q \, E) \( O \) - "ref"_(t^0) \( R_(q^0 \, E) \( O \) \) I \,\
s_q \( O \) & = "ref"_t \( cal(D)_(q \, 3) \( O \) \) - "ref"_(t^0) \( cal(D)_(q^0 \, 3) \( O \) \) . $
  The references in the second line use the same filled state.
    Lemma 25, first on common northern slots and then on rapid tails by norm approximation, therefore gives
  $ s_q \( O \) = "ref"_(t^0) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cal(D)_(q \, 3) \( O \) -
    cal(D)_(q^0 \, 3) \( O \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + O \( q^(- D) \) . $
  The bounded-flux comparison in Lemma 24 supplies the additional factor $q^(- 1)$.
    Substituting these scalar identities into the branch comparison yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.13)")
   $ J_(q \, E)^(*) \( O - "ref"_q \( O \) \) J_(q \, E) =  & \( O - "ref"_t \( O \) \) +
     #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cal(D)_(q \, 3) \( O \) - "ref"_t \( cal(D)_(q \, 3) \( O \) \)
     #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]\
 & + s_q \( O \) I + R'_(q \, E) \( O \) \,\
\| s_q \( O \) \| lt.eq  & C r^c u \/ q^3 + C_D q^(- D) \, wide parallel R'_(q \, E) \( O \) parallel lt.eq
  C_D q^(- D) . $ <eq:flags-centered-E>]
  Here and below the symbols denote compressions to the relevant child zero space.
    When $h = 0$ the comparison is its own filled calibration, so all centered contributions vanish exactly.
  For an $F$ step, $t = q - 1$. The nearest smaller filled fluxes are either identical or differ by three.
    In the latter case apply the filled $E$ comparison just used. Together with (5.8), this proves
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.14)")
   $ J_(q \, F)^(*) \( O - "ref"_q \( O \) \) J_(q \, F) = O - "ref"_t \( O \) + S_(q \, F) \( O \) \, wide
     parallel S_(q \, F) \( O \) parallel lt.eq C r^c u \/ q^2 + C_D q^(- D) . $ <eq:flags-centered-F>]
  The off-diagonal blocks have norm $C_D q^(- D)$. An $F$ step already records a flag,
    so its entire error will be charged to that flag.
  Here is a uniform closure argument, including the parameter choices. Let $c \, b_0$ be as in Lemma 24.
    Choose $b > max { b_0 \, c }$, then choose $B > max { a b \,
    c }$ large enough to absorb the fixed polynomial costs of the local calculus.
    A constant $M$ will be fixed after $B$. Set
  $ tau = M r^b u . $
  If $q lt.eq tau$ and $h > 0$, every flag has $b' lt.eq q$, so
  $ W_m gt.eq \( 2 M r^b \)^(- a) I . $
  The norm bound $parallel O - "ref"_q \( O \) parallel lt.eq
    2$ proves (5.12) in this region with constant $2 \( 2 M \)^a$. When $h = 0$ the centered compression is zero.
    Increasing $M$ includes all stopping cases in this argument.
  Fix a finite $Q$, and let $K_Q$ be the least constant in (5.12) for fluxes at most $Q$, with the chosen $B$,
    all number sectors, and all allowed frames. This number is finite:
    for $h > 0$ use $W_m gt.eq \( 1 + Q \)^(- a)$, while the filled compression is exactly centered. We prove
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.15)")
   $ K_Q lt.eq C_M + 1 / 2 K_Q $ <eq:flags-bootstrap-constant>]
  with $C_M$ independent of $Q$.
  Suppose $q > tau$. Expand the unchanged $O$ down the branching tree, using (5.13) and (5.14),
    until the current flux is at most $tau$. The original northern slots, center,
    and radius are unchanged through this part of the descent. The terminal comparisons have just been bounded.
    In an $E$ child, decompose $cal(D)_(q \, 3) \( O \)$ into its core and dyadic ball shells,
    all centered at $m$. Equation (4.13), with a tail order larger than $B + 2$, gives
  $ sum_nu r_nu^B parallel cal(D)_(q \, 3) \( O \)_nu parallel lt.eq C \( B \) r^(B + c) u \/ q^2 . $
  Applying the definition of $K_Q$ to each smaller-flux shell, and using linearity of the reference,
    bounds its centered contribution by
  $ K_Q C \( B \) r^(B + c) u \/ q^2 $
  times the remaining history weight in that child. Along any word, the flux strictly decreases; thus
  $ sum_(q' > tau) frac(u, \( q' \)^2) lt.eq C u / tau = frac(C, M r^b) . $
  Consequently all induction-dependent contributions are bounded by
  $ K_Q frac(C \( B \), M) r^(B + c - b) W_m . $
  Choose $M$ so large that this is at most $1 / 2 K_Q r^B W_m$. This choice is possible because $b > c$ and $r gt.eq 2$.
  We verify that the other errors have a bound independent of $K_Q$.
    At a node with positive remaining hole number, its identity is bounded by the sum of the identities assigned
    to its remaining flags. A scalar electron error may therefore be charged to every such flag.
    For a flag at $ell$, the total charge from ancestors in (5.13) is at most
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.16)")
   $ C frac(r^c u, max { tau \, 1 + ell }^2) . $ <eq:flags-scalar-tail>]
  The $F$ error in (5.14) occurs at its own flag and obeys the same bound, up to a fixed constant.
    Write $w_ell = min { 1 \, \( u \/ \( 1 + ell \) \)^a }$. Since $a < 2$ and $tau = M r^b u$,
  $ frac(r^c u, max { tau \, 1 + ell }^2) lt.eq C M^(a - 2) r^(c + b \( a - 2 \)) w_ell lt.eq C_M r^B w_ell . $
  The $q^(- D)$ remainders, including the off-diagonal blocks, obey the same estimate after choosing $D > a + 2$.
    For a self-adjoint off-diagonal block, its norm bounds the full block matrix above and below by that norm
    times the identity at its node, so the same charge applies.
    Nodes with $h = 0$ contribute nothing, by the exact centered filled identity.
    Summation of these diagonal bounds along words proves (5.15).
  It follows that $K_Q lt.eq 2 C_M$ for every $Q$, proving the uniform claim.
    Finally, the assertion for $A = sum_nu A_nu$ follows by linearity of the reference and by summing the form
    inequalities. ◻
]

The theorem estimates a compression to the zero spaces. It does not claim that ordinary local fluctuations vanish
  in the filled state. It also applies to coherent superpositions of histories:
  all charges above are diagonal operator bounds, rather than bounds conditional on a classical choice of word.

=== 5.5 Rotation averaging in a one-hole space <rotation-averaging-in-a-one-hole-space>

The history estimate still depends on a chosen readout axis.
  In this subsection, a one-hole space means a space with quasihole degree $h = 1$.
  Representation theory then removes the axis dependence and yields an integrated estimate with no factor of the
  sphere area.

#thm([Corollary 5.7.])[
  (One-hole comparison).  #emph[Suppose $h = 1$, equivalently $q = 3 n - 2$.
    Let $psi \, phi.alt$ be any two unit vectors in $Z_(n \, q)$.
    Let ${ O_x }$ be a measurable family of neutral self-adjoint observables of norm at most one,
    supported in balls of a fixed radius $r gt.eq 2$ about $x$, with center measure of bounded density.
    There are constants $C \, B' < oo$, independent of $n \, q$, such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.17)")
 $ integral lr(|chevron.l psi \, O_x psi chevron.r - chevron.l phi.alt \, O_x phi.alt chevron.r|) thin d x lt.eq
   C r^(B') . $ <eq:flags-one-hole>]

 The same conclusion holds for rapidly localized families,
   with the right side bounded by the corresponding fixed rapid-locality seminorm.
   For a family whose norms are bounded by $A$, multiply the right side by $A$.]
] <cor:flags-one-hole>

#proof[
  Choose $a in \( 1 \, 2 \)$ in Theorem 26. The symmetric-polynomial factor in (5.2) has degree one in each
    variable. It is the irreducible spin-$n \/ 2$ representation,
    so $Z_(n \, q)$ is irreducible and has dimension $n + 1$.
  When $h = 1$, an $F$ branch has $h = 0$ and hence dimension one.
    Before that branch all steps are $E$, which lower the flux by three.
    Therefore at most one one-dimensional history has a flag at any specified nonterminal flux.
    The history that reaches the stopping region without an $F$ step has bounded terminal dimension.
    It follows that, as an operator on $Z_(n \, q)$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.18)")
   $ "Tr" \( W_0 \) lt.eq C + sum_(b gt.eq 0) \( 1 + b \)^(- a) lt.eq C_a . $ <eq:flags-one-hole-trace>]
  The trace is unchanged on conjugating back to the physical zero space.
    Write $hat(W)_0 = cal(R)_(n \, q)^(*) W_0 cal(R)_(n \, q)$ for that physical operator.
  Let $V_q \( g \)$ denote the rotation representation, with normalized Haar measure $d g$. Schur’s lemma gives
  $ integral_(upright("SU") \( 2 \)) V_q \( g \) hat(W)_0 V_q \( g \)^(*) thin d g =
    frac("Tr" \( hat(W)_0 \), n + 1) I . $
  For each rotation use the readout axis whose north pole is the image of the original north pole.
    Precisely, write $x \( g \) = g dot.op upright("north")$ and $W^g = V_q \( g \) hat(W)_0 V_q \( g \)^(*)$.
    Use the rotated frame and rotated branch construction at $x \( g \)$. Theorem 26, applied to $O_(x \( g \))$, gives
  $ \| chevron.l psi \, O_(x \( g \)) psi chevron.r - chevron.l phi.alt \,
    O_(x \( g \)) phi.alt chevron.r #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|] lt.eq
    C r^(B') #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] chevron.l psi \, W^g psi chevron.r + chevron.l phi.alt \,
    W^g phi.alt chevron.r #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  The two reference expectations cancel in this inequality.
    Changes between local frames cost only a fixed polynomial in $r$ by the local calculus.
    Integrating rotations and multiplying by the sphere area yields at most $C r^(B') q
    "Tr" \( W_0 \) \/ \( n + 1 \)$, which is uniformly bounded because $q = 3 n - 2$.
    For any center measure with bounded mass in unit balls, spread the mass at each center over a ball of fixed
    radius. The resulting density relative to area is bounded uniformly.
    The operator originally assigned to $x$ is supported in a ball of radius $r +
    1$ about every point in that spreading ball, so the same pointwise bound applies before integration.
    This reduces the general center measure to the area estimate.
    Finally, summing ball shells proves the assertion for rapid families. ◻
]

== 6 Charge bounds and rotation averaging <sec:charge>

The history estimate of Theorem 26 controls a local observable by weighted flags.
  To treat arbitrary hole number by rotation averaging, we compare these weights with a physical one-body
  observable, first with a slowly decreasing weight and then with a summable weight.

Increase the fixed stopping flux in the history construction, if necessary,
  so that it is at least eight and all pinning comparisons above it are valid, including shifts by at most three.
  Write $q_(upright(s)) = q_(upright("stop"))$ and let $cal(R)_(n \, q)$ denote the history unitary in (5.9).
  For $L gt.eq 2$ and $0 < p < 2$, put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.1)")
 $ f \( x \) = f_(L \, p) \( x \) = (frac(L, L + x))^p \, wide C_q \( f \) =
   frac(q + 3, 3 \( q + 1 \)) sum_(j = 0)^q f \( j \) \,\
S_(L \, p) \( omega \) = sum_(b in cal(B) \( omega \)) f \( b \) . $ <eq:charge-definitions>]

When $q$ is divisible by three, $C_q \( f \)$ is the filled-state expectation of $D_q \( f \)$:
  rotational invariance gives occupation $\( q + 3 \) \/ \( 3 \( q + 1 \) \)$ in every orbital.
  The formula defines the scalar $C_q \( f \)$ also at the other fluxes.
  The operator $S_(L \, p)$ is diagonal in histories and scalar on each terminal summand.
  Its sum is over the flag multiset $cal(B) \( omega \)$, including all terminal flags at position zero;
  each history has exactly $h = q + 3 - 3 n$ flags. All operator inequalities involving $S_(L \, p)$ below are in
  history coordinates. In particular, they make no measurement assumption about flags in a zero-mode vector.

#thm([Proposition 6.1.])[
  (Pooled charge).  #emph[For each of $p = 1 \/ 8$ and $p = 3 \/ 2$ there are fixed $L gt.eq
    2$ and $c > 0$ such that, for every flux and every nonzero zero-mode space $Z_(n \, q)$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.2)")
 $ cal(R)_(n \, q) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] C_q \( f \) upright(bold(1)) -
   P_(n \, q) D_q \( f \) P_(n \, q) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] cal(R)_(n \, q)^(*) med gt.eq med
   c S_(L \, p) . $ <eq:charge-pooled>]

 Here compression to $Z_(n \, q)$ is understood, and $P_(n \, q)$ denotes its orthogonal projection.
   The constants are independent of $q \, n$ and of the readout axis. When $h = 0$, both sides vanish.]
] <prop:charge-pooled>

The obstruction to this estimate occurs at electron steps.
  Along a history, each F step contributes approximately $- f \( q \) \/ 3$ to $D_q \( f \) -
  C_q \( f \) upright(bold(1))$, supplying a credit for its new flag.
  Each E step contributes a positive scalar correction. When at least two flags remain,
  divide this scalar cost between them. The scalar tail estimate below leaves the strictly positive margin

$ 1 / 3 - frac(1 + p, 6 \( 1 - p \)) = 5 / 42 wide \( p = 1 \/ 8 \) . $

With one remaining flag, there is no such margin. We instead combine the scalar with the transported observable
  and compare it to a filled step; the one-hole comparison of Corollary 27 makes the resulting error summably
  small. These two cases prove the first charge estimate.

Rotation averaging then bounds integrated local-expectation differences between two zero-mode states by
  $C \( q + 1 \)^(7 \/ 8)$ times the sum of their hole numbers.
  This still grows with the area, but it is enough to prove the charge estimate again with $p = 3 \/ 2$.
  For a polar annulus $1 + m asymp M$, use this averaged bound while the flux is moderate relative to $M$;
  the transport interaction has a factor $\( q + 1 \)^(- 2)$ that pays for the area dependence.
  At larger flux, apply the original fixed-axis history estimate directly to the annulus.
  The two resulting sums are small when the new weight scale $L$ is large.
  The summable weight then gives the area-independent local-expectation bound at the end of this section.

=== 6.1 Scalar telescoping and summable error bounds <scalar-telescoping-and-summable-error-bounds>

We estimate $D_q \( f \) - C_q \( f \) upright(bold(1))$ from above.
  An F step contributes the scalar $C_(q - 1) \( f \) - C_q \( f \)$, and an E step contributes

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.3)")
 $ ell_q = C_(q - 3) \( f \) + f \( q \) - C_q \( f \) . $ <eq:charge-electron-scalar>]

For the continuous comparison define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.4)")
 $ F \( x \) = 1 / x integral_0^x f \( t \) thin d t \, wide ell \( x \) = - 2 F' \( x \) +
   f' \( x \) quad \( x > 0 \) \, $ <eq:charge-continuous-scalar>]

with the continuous extensions at zero.

#thm([Lemma 6.2.])[
  (Scalar estimates).  #emph[Fix $p in \( 0 \, 2 \)$ and a lower stopping flux $q_(upright(s)) gt.eq 8$.
    There is a function $epsilon_p \( L \) arrow.r 0$ as $L arrow.r oo$ with the following properties,
    uniformly for $q gt.eq q_(upright(s))$:
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.5)")
 $ lr(|C_(q - 1) \( f \) - C_q \( f \) + 1 / 3 f \( q \)|) & lt.eq epsilon_p \( L \) f \( q \) \,\
sum_(q gt.eq q_(upright(s))) frac(\| ell_q - ell_(3 floor.l q \/ 3 floor.r) \|, f \( q \)) & lt.eq
  epsilon_p \( L \) . $ <eq:charge-F-scalar>]

 The function $ell$ is nonnegative. If $0 < p < 1$, then for every collection $cal(E)$ of E-step fluxes along a
   history, and every flag position $k$ below these steps,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.6)")
 $ sum_(q in cal(E)\
q gt.eq k) \( ell_q \)_(+) lt.eq (frac(1 + p, 3 \( 1 - p \)) + epsilon_p \( L \)) f \( k \) . $ <eq:charge-scalar-tail>]

 Changing $k$ by a fixed bounded amount, or taking $k$ in the terminal range, only changes $epsilon_p \( L \)$.]
] <lem:charge-scalars>

#proof[
  The exact formula
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.7)")
   $ ell_q = f \( q \) - (1 + frac(2, q + 1)) frac(f \( q \) + f \( q - 1 \) + f \( q - 2 \), 3) +
     frac(2 sum_(j = 0)^(q - 3) f \( j \), \( q + 1 \) \( q - 2 \)) $ <eq:charge-scalar-exact>]
  is obtained by separating the last three summands in $C_q$.
    A useful positive form of this identity is obtained by putting $d_j = f \( j \) - 2 f \( j + 1 \) +
    f \( j + 2 \)$ and $Q_q = \( q + 1 \) \( q - 2 \)$:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.8)")
   $ ell_q = 1 / Q_q sum_(j = 0)^(q - 3) \( j + 1 \) \( j + 2 \) d_j +
     frac(2 q, 3 \( q + 1 \)) d_(q - 2) . $ <eq:charge-positive-scalar>]
  Expanding the second differences verifies the formula coefficient by coefficient.
    In particular, $ell_q gt.eq 0$, and constant and affine functions contribute zero.
  Here are precise discrete-to-continuous error bounds:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.9)")
   $ \| ell_q - ell \( q \) \| + sup_(x in \[ q - 3 \, q \]) \| ell \( x \) - ell \( q \) \| & lt.eq
     C \( L + q \)^(- 2) \,\
\| ell_q - ell_(q - r) \| & lt.eq
  cases(delim: "{", C L^(- 2) \, & q lt.eq L \,, C \( F \( q \) + f \( q \) \) q^(- 2) \, & q > L \,)
  $ <eq:charge-scalar-errors>]
  for $r = 0 \, 1 \, 2$. We give details because the sharper second estimate is needed for the summable weight.
    The identity $d_j = integral \( 1 - \| t - j - 1 \| \)_(+) f'' \( t \) thin d
    t$ expresses (6.8) as $integral_0^q kappa_q \( t \) f'' \( t \) thin d t$.
    The function $kappa_q$ is linear between consecutive integers and has values
  $ kappa_q \( i \) = frac(i \( i + 1 \), Q_q) med \( 0 lt.eq i lt.eq q - 2 \) \, wide kappa_q \( q - 1 \) =
    frac(2 q, 3 \( q + 1 \)) \, wide kappa_q \( q \) = 0 . $
  On $\[ 0 \, q - 2 \]$ its difference from $t^2 \/ q^2$ is at most $C \( t \/ q^2 + t^2 \/ q^3 \)$,
    and on $\[ q - 2 \, q \]$ that difference is bounded by a constant.
    The error in its integral against $f''$ is consequently at most
  $ C
    (q^(- 2) integral_0^q t f'' \( t \) thin d t + q^(- 3) integral_0^q t^2 f'' \( t \) thin d t + integral_(q -
    2)^q f'' \( t \) thin d t) . $
  For $q lt.eq L$ use $f'' lt.eq C \/ L^2$; for $q > L$ use $integral_0^q t f'' lt.eq 1$,
    $integral_0^q t^2 f'' lt.eq 2 q F \( q \)$, and $f'' \( t \) lt.eq C f \( t \) \( L + t \)^(- 2)$.
    This proves the first term of (6.9). The continuous identity $ell \( x \) =
    x^(- 2) integral_0^x t^2 f'' \( t \) thin d t$ implies $ell' \( x \) = f'' \( x \) - 2 ell \( x \) \/ x$,
    which proves the oscillation estimate.
  For (6.9), difference the positive formula directly:
  $ ell_q - ell_(q - 1) =  & - frac(2 \( q - 1 \), Q_q Q_(q - 1)) sum_(j = 0)^(q - 4) \( j + 1 \) \( j + 2 \) d_j\
 & + frac(\( q - 1 \) \( q - 2 \), 3 q \( q + 1 \)) d_(q - 3) + frac(2 q, 3 \( q + 1 \)) d_(q - 2) . $
  The first sum is bounded by $C q F \( q \)$, as follows by its integral representation and $integral_0^q t^2 f''
    lt.eq 2 q F \( q \)$. This gives $C F \( q \) \/ q^2$ when $q > L$;
    the last two terms cost $C f \( q \) \/ q^2$. Below $L$ use $d_j lt.eq C \/ L^2$ in the displayed formula.
    A bounded number of differences proves the claimed bound.
  The exact F-step identity is
  $ C_(q - 1) \( f \) - C_q \( f \) + 1 / 3 f \( q \) = frac(2, 3 q \( q + 1 \)) sum_(j = 0)^(q - 1)
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] f \( j \) - f \( q \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  For $q lt.eq L$ its ratio to $f \( q \)$ is $O \( L^(- 1) \)$. For $q > L$ use
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.10)")
   $ F \( q \) lt.eq cases(delim: "{", f \( q \) \/ \( 1 - p \) \, & p < 1 \,, C_p L \/ q \, & p > 1 .)
     $ <eq:charge-average-weight>]
  The resulting ratio is $O \( L^(- 1) \)$ also when $1 < p < 2$, since $L^(1 - p) q^(p - 2) lt.eq
    L^(- 1)$ for $q gt.eq L$. The case $p = 1$ follows from the explicit logarithmic integral and is not needed
    below. Dividing (6.9) by $f \( q \)$ and summing gives $O \( L^(- 1) \)$ below $L$.
    Above $L$ it gives $O \( L^(- 1) \)$: the summands are $O \( q^(- 2) \)$ for $p <
    1$ and $O \( q^(- 2) + L^(1 - p) q^(p - 3) \)$ for $1 < p < 2$.
  Twice integrating $f''$ proves
  $ ell \( x \) = 1 / x^2 integral_0^x t^2 f'' \( t \) thin d t gt.eq 0 \, wide
    integral_k^oo ell \( x \) thin d x = 2 F \( k \) - f \( k \) . $
  The E-step intervals $\[ q - 3 \, q \]$ have disjoint interiors along a history.
    Consequently their continuous contribution is at most $1 /
    3 integral_(\( k - 3 \)_(+))^oo ell \( x \) thin d x$. For $p < 1$,
    (6.10) bounds this by $frac(1 + p, 3 \( 1 - p \)) f \( k \)$,
    up to $O \( L^(- 1) \) f \( k \)$ from the bounded endpoint shift.
    Finally, the tail sum of (6.9) is bounded by $C \/ \( L + k \)$, and
  $ frac(1, L + k) lt.eq 1 / L f \( k \) wide \( 0 < p < 1 \) . $
  Thus it is $O \( L^(- 1) \) f \( k \)$ uniformly in $k$. This proves (6.6). ◻
]

We next list the analytic error sums that will be used in both charge arguments.
  Retain the size function $A_q$ from Proposition 18. With $u = 1 + m$, $v = 1 + q - m$, and $w =
  sqrt(u v \/ \( q + 1 \))$, it satisfies

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.11)")
 $ A_q \( m \) = frac(u^2 f'' \( m \), \( q + 1 \)^2) asymp frac(f \( m \), \( q + 1 \)^2) (frac(u, L + u))^2 \,\
B_q = integral_0^q A_q \( m \) thin d m \, wide J_q = integral_0^q frac(A_q \( m \), v) thin d m . $ <eq:charge-size>]

The longitude integral has bounded mass and is included in constants.
  Proposition 18 gives this size for each transported change in $D \( f \)$.

#thm([Lemma 6.3.])[
  (Uniform error sums).  #emph[For either $p = 1 \/ 8$ or $p = 3 \/ 2$, as $L arrow.r oo$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.12)")
 $ sup_(q gt.eq q_(upright(s))) frac(B_q, f \( q \)) = O \( L^(- 1) \) \, wide
   sum_(q gt.eq q_(upright(s))) (frac(J_q, f \( q \)) + frac(1, \( L + q \)^2)) =
   O (frac(1 + log L, L)) . $ <eq:charge-common-sums>]

 For $p = 1 \/ 8$ there are, in addition,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.13)")
 $ sum_(q gt.eq q_(upright(s))) frac(sup_(0 lt.eq m lt.eq q) A_q \( m \), f \( q \)) = O \( L^(- 1) \) \, wide
   sum_(q gt.eq q_(upright(s))) frac(1, f \( q \)) integral_0^q frac(A_q \( m \), w) thin d m =
   O \( L^(- 1 \/ 2) \) . $ <eq:charge-first-sums>]

 All implied constants are independent of $L$.]
] <lem:charge-sums>

#proof[
  For $q lt.eq L$, integrating $u^2 \/ L^2$ gives $B_q lt.eq C \( q + 1 \) L^(- 2)$; for $q > L$,
    dropping the factor $\( u \/ \( L + u \) \)^2$ gives $B_q lt.eq C F \( q \) \/ q$.
    These estimates and (6.10) prove the first bound in (6.12).
    On the northern half of the sphere $v gt.eq \( q + 1 \) \/ 2$, whereas on the southern half $A_q \( m \) lt.eq
    C f \( q \) \( L + q \)^(- 2)$. Therefore
  $ frac(J_q, f \( q \)) lt.eq frac(C B_q, \( q + 1 \) f \( q \)) + frac(C log \( q + 2 \), \( L + q \)^2) . $
  The first term sums to $O \( L^(- 1) \)$: above $L$ it is bounded by $C q^(- 2)$ for $p < 1$ and by $C L^(1 - p)
    q^(p - 3)$ for $p > 1$. The second sums to $O \( \( 1 + log L \) \/ L \)$.
  For the supremum in (6.13), use $C L^(- 2)$ when $q lt.eq L$ and $C \( q + 1 \)^(- 2)$ otherwise.
    The latter divided by $f \( q \)$ is $O \( L^(- p) q^(p - 2) \)$, whose sum is $O \( L^(- 1) \)$ for $p < 1$.
    On the northern half, $w gt.eq c sqrt(u)$, so the last integral is at most
  $ frac(C, \( q + 1 \)^2) integral_0^(q \/ 2) frac(f \( m \) u^(3 \/ 2), \( L + u \)^2) thin d m +
    frac(C sqrt(q + 1) thin f \( q \), \( L + q \)^2) . $
  After division by $f \( q \)$, both terms are bounded by $C sqrt(q + 1) \/ L^2$ for $q lt.eq
    L$ and by $C q^(- 3 \/ 2)$ for $q > L$, provided $p < 1 \/ 2$. Their sums are $O \( L^(- 1 \/ 2) \)$. ◻
]

=== 6.2 Calibration and the first charge estimate <calibration-and-the-first-charge-estimate>

Set $K_q := K_(q \, 3) \( f \)$ on $cal(F)_(q - 3)$. Its compression to $Z_(n - 1 \, q - 3)$ is the transported
  change in an E step from $Z_(n \, q)$, as in Proposition 23. Its center terms have size $A_q \( m \)$.
  A direct estimate of $ell_q$ would accumulate a scalar even in a filled state.
  We instead calibrate $K_q + ell_q$ by a filled step at the nearest lower flux divisible by three.

Put $q^0 = 3 floor.l q \/ 3 floor.r$ and $r = q - q^0 in { 0 \, 1 \, 2 }$.
  Start with the filled state at flux $q^0 - 3$ and increase the flux $r$ times by F transport at the same south
  pole. The resulting unit vector $chi_q$ lies at flux $q - 3$ and has the minimum possible hole number $r$.
  If $r = 0$, it is the filled state itself.

#thm([Lemma 6.4.])[
  (Calibrated electron step).  #emph[For $q$ above the fixed stopping range,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.14)")
 $ lr(|ell_q + chevron.l K_q chevron.r_(chi_q)|) lt.eq
   C (J_q + \| ell_q - ell_(q^0) \| + frac(f \( q \), \( L + q \)^2)) . $ <eq:charge-calibrated-scalar>]

 For any collection of northern center terms of $K_q$, their expectation in $chi_q$ may instead be replaced by the
   linear reference functional of Theorem 26 at child flux $q - 3$; the additional error is at most $C J_q$.
   This remains true when the choice between the two centerings is made separately on disjoint annuli.]
] <lem:charge-calibration>

#proof[
  In the filled E step at $q^0$, both parent and child compressions of $D \( f \) -
    C \( f \) upright(bold(1))$ vanish. Proposition 23 therefore gives $\| ell_(q^0) +
    chevron.l K_(q^0) chevron.r \| lt.eq C f \( q^0 \) \( L + q^0 \)^(- 2)$.
    Corollary 20 compares this filled-step expectation first with the directly embedded lower-flux state and then
    with its at most two F-growth transports. Its bound is $C J_q$ for each comparison,
    proving (6.14) after adding $\| ell_q - ell_(q^0) \|$.
  For a northern source center, the linear reference functional of Theorem 26, applied at child flux $q - 3$,
    is the expectation in the same directly embedded filled state at $q^0 - 3$, using the common slots.
    The last assertion of Corollary 20 applies to any selected collection of source pieces and bounds the sum of
    absolute comparison errors by $C J_q$. It therefore also permits the annulus-dependent choice of centering in
    the statement. ◻
]

For completeness, the elementary rule for summing history bounds is as follows.
  At a node of flux $q$ with positive hole number, every descendant flag is at an index at most $q$, and hence

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.15)")
 $ S_(L \, p) gt.eq h f \( q \) upright(bold(1)) gt.eq f \( q \) upright(bold(1)) $ <eq:charge-node-lower-bound>]

on that node’s full descendant space. A node error bounded by $eta_q f \( q \)
  upright(bold(1))$ can consequently be replaced by $eta_q S_(L \, p)$ there.
  If $sum_q eta_q lt.eq eta$, the sum of all these node errors is at most $eta S_(L \, p)$:
  on each history, each flag appears only at ancestor fluxes, each visited at most once.
  This is a comparison of diagonal operators after the node form bounds have been applied.
  It also controls an off-diagonal remainder by its operator norm.
  At a node with no holes, use the exact vanishing of the centered compression instead of this rule.

#proof[
  Apply Proposition 23 recursively to $D_q \( f \) - C_q \( f \) upright(bold(1))$.
    The norm remainder at a node is at most $C f \( q \) \( L + q \)^(- 2)$,
    and its total cost is $o_L \( 1 \) S_(L \, p)$ by (6.15) and Lemma 30. F transport has norm at most $C B_q$.
    Together with (6.5), its contribution is
  $ - #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 / 3 - o_L \( 1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] f \( q \) $
  for the flag created at that step.
  At an E step with one remaining hole, $q equiv 1 med \( mod med 3 \)$, so $chi_q$ also has one hole.
    Compare the child vector to $chi_q$ using Corollary 27. The difference of the $K_q$ expectations is at most $C
    sup_m A_q \( m \)$. This is a two-sided form bound since it holds for every unit vector in the child space.
    The calibration error and all one-hole errors have total cost $o_L \( 1 \) S_(L \, p)$ by Lemmas 29 and 30.
  At an E step with at least two remaining holes, Proposition 19 bounds the increase from $K_q$ by $C integral
    A_q \( m \) \/ w thin d m$. Its total cost is again $o_L \( 1 \) S_(L \, p)$.
    It remains to pay the positive part of $ell_q$. Divide that scalar equally among the remaining flags.
    A fixed flag then pays at most one half of each $\( ell_q \)_(+)$ at such an ancestor.
    The E-step intervals are disjoint, so Lemma 29 bounds the total payment for a flag at $k$ by
  $ (frac(1 + p, 6 \( 1 - p \)) + o_L \( 1 \)) f \( k \) = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 3 / 14 +
    o_L \( 1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] f \( k \) . $
  At terminal fluxes there are only finitely many spaces. Uniformly on them, $f \( j \) = 1 + O \( L^(- 1) \)$,
    and hence
  $ D_q \( f \) - C_q \( f \) upright(bold(1)) = - h / 3 upright(bold(1)) + O \( L^(- 1) \) . $
  For $h = 0$ the expression is exactly zero by rotational invariance; for $h gt.eq
    1$ the error is $O \( L^(- 1) \) h$, which is also $O \( L^(- 1) \)$ times the terminal flag weight.
    Terminal flags therefore receive the same credit $1 \/ 3 - o_L \( 1 \)$. Combining all bounds yields
  $ cal(R)_(n \, q) \( D_q \( f \) - C_q \( f \) upright(bold(1)) \) cal(R)_(n \, q)^(*) lt.eq -
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 / 3 - 3 / 14 - o_L \( 1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] S_(L \, p) . $
  The strict margin is $5 \/ 42$. Choose $L = L_0$ so that the total error is less than $5 \/ 84$ and take $c =
    5 \/ 84$. ◻
]

We record what this first estimate gives after rotation averaging.
  It will be applied to annular portions of the next transport interaction, with their center size factored out.

#thm([Corollary 6.5.])[
  (First averaged comparison).  #emph[Let $W$ be a neutral Hermitian interaction with bounded strength per unit
    area and fixed rapid locality bounds. For unit vectors $psi in Z_(n \, q)$ and $chi in
    Z_(n' \, q)$ with hole numbers $h \, h'$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.16)")
 $ \| chevron.l W chevron.r_psi - chevron.l W chevron.r_chi \| lt.eq
   C_W \( q + 1 \)^(7 \/ 8) \( h + h' \) . $ <eq:charge-weak-average>]

 For radius-$r$ interactions of unit local norm one can take $C_W lt.eq C \( 1 + r \)^B$ for a fixed $B$.]
] <cor:charge-weak-average>

#proof[
  To specify the rotation average on a physical zero space,
    let $V_(n \, q) \( g \)$ denote its rotation representation and define
  $ S_(L \, p)^g = V_(n \, q) \( g \) cal(R)_(n \, q)^(*) S_(L \, p) cal(R)_(n \, q) V_(n \, q) \( g \)^(*) . $
  Thus $S_(L \, p)^g$ acts on $Z_(n \, q)$, whereas $S_(L \, p)$ itself acts in history coordinates.
    This is the history construction obtained by rotating a fixed readout frame by $g$;
    its estimates are uniform in $g$. Use this convention separately in each number sector.
  For a local term centered at $x \( g \) = g dot.op upright("north")$,
    use Theorem 26 in the frame rotated by $g$, with exponent $a = 7 \/ 4$.
    The local term itself need not belong to a rotation-covariant family; the estimate is pointwise in $g$.
    Since $\( 1 + b \)^(- a) lt.eq C f_(L_0 \, 1 \/ 8) \( b \)$,
    the local comparison is bounded by the corresponding rotated $S_(L_0 \, 1 \/ 8)$.
    Its reference scalar cancels between the two vectors. By irreducibility of $U_q$,
    normalized rotation averaging gives the one-particle identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.17)")
   $ integral_(S U \( 2 \)) D_q \( f \)^g thin d g = frac(sum_(j = 0)^q f \( j \), q + 1) thin cal(N) \,
     $ <eq:charge-rotation-average>]
  where Haar measure has mass one. Proposition 28 for $p = 1 \/ 8$ consequently gives
  $ integral_(S U \( 2 \)) chevron.l S_(L_0 \, 1 \/ 8)^g chevron.r_psi thin d g lt.eq
    frac(h, 3 c \( q + 1 \)) sum_(j = 0)^q f_(L_0 \, 1 \/ 8) \( j \) . $
  The physical area is $2 pi q$, and the center density is bounded.
    Multiplying by that area and using $sum_(j = 0)^q f_(L_0 \, 1 \/ 8) \( j \) lt.eq
    C \( q + 1 \)^(7 \/ 8)$ proves the result. The same argument applies to any center measure with bounded mass
    in unit balls: spread each center uniformly over a ball of fixed radius and regard its observable as centered
    at the new point. The resulting measure has bounded area density,
    and the support radius increases by only a fixed constant.
    Thus the preceding integral estimate applies to both discrete and continuous center measures.
    Finally, a rapid shell expansion multiplies the radius-$r$ estimate by a summable series of shell norms times
    $\( 1 + r \)^B$. ◻
]

=== 6.3 The summable weight <the-summable-weight>

We now fix $p = 3 \/ 2$, keeping the already chosen $L_0$ and the constant in Corollary 32 fixed.
  The new scale $L$ will be chosen at the end. Calibrate every E step with positive hole number by Lemma 31.
  Its scalar error, the branch remainders, and all F-step errors have total cost $o_L \( 1 \)
  S_(L \, 3 \/ 2)$ by the estimates already proved. It remains to bound the centered E interactions.

Divide centers into annuli $M lt.eq 1 + m < 2 M$, where $M = 1 \, 2 \, 4 \, dots.h$. Write

$ a_M = (frac(M, L + M))^2 \, wide f_M = f \( M \) \, wide delta = 1 / 24 . $

On this annulus the local interaction size is at most $C \( q + 1 \)^(- 2) f_M a_M$.
  Choose a fixed sufficiently large $C_0$ and separate fluxes at $T_M = C_0 M^(1 + delta)$.
  An annulus is present only if $q + 1 gt.eq M$.

#proof[
  First consider $q lt.eq T_M$. Center the annular interaction at $chi_q$.
    If the child has $h > 0$ holes, the calibration state has $r lt.eq h$ holes, so Corollary 32,
    applied at child flux $q - 3$, bounds the centered interaction in absolute quadratic form by
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.18)")
   $ C \( q + 1 \)^(- 2) f_M a_M \( q + 1 \)^(7 \/ 8) h upright(bold(1)) . $ <eq:charge-near-cost>]
  The child flag weight is at least $h f \( q \)$. For $M lt.eq q + 1 lt.eq T_M + 1$, $f_M \/ f \( q \) lt.eq
    C M^(p delta)$. Thus summing the relative cost (6.18) over these fluxes gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.19)")
   $ C a_M M^(p delta) sum_(q + 1 gt.eq M) \( q + 1 \)^(- 1 - 1 \/ 8) lt.eq C a_M M^(- \( 1 \/ 8 - p delta \)) =
     C a_M M^(- 1 \/ 16) . $ <eq:charge-near-sum>]
  The finitely many fluxes near the stopping range obey the same estimate after increasing its constant.
  Now let $q > T_M$. With $C_0$ sufficiently large, the annulus lies in the common northern region.
    Center it by the linear reference functional in Theorem 26.
    Lemma 31 accounts for this change of centering with the already budgeted error $C J_q$.
    For a center in the annulus, each descendant history $omega$ satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.20)")
   $ W_m \( omega \) & lt.eq C sum_(k in cal(B) \( omega \)) min (1 \, (frac(M, 1 + k))^a)\
 & lt.eq C / f_M sum_(k in cal(B) \( omega \)) f \( k \) = C /
   f_M S_(L \, 3 \/ 2) \( omega \) . $ <eq:charge-weight-comparison>]
  This is also a diagonal operator inequality in the descendant history space.
    Indeed, for $k lt.eq M$, $f \( k \) gt.eq f \( M \)$; for $k > M$, use $a =
    7 \/ 4 > p$ and $M \/ \( 1 + k \) lt.eq C \( L + M \) \/ \( L + k \)$. The annulus has area $O \( M \)$.
    The local readout estimate therefore bounds its centered interaction by
  $ C \( q + 1 \)^(- 2) f_M a_M M / f_M S_(L \, 3 \/ 2) . $
  For rapid interactions this formula follows by applying the radius-$r$ bound to each shell and summing its norm
    times $\( 1 + r \)^B$. The shell center stays in the same annulus even when its support extends beyond it;
    shells reaching the south are covered by the large-radius part of the readout estimate. Summing over $q > T_M$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.21)")
   $ C a_M M sum_(q > T_M) \( q + 1 \)^(- 2) S_(L \, 3 \/ 2) lt.eq
     C a_M M^(- delta) S_(L \, 3 \/ 2) . $ <eq:charge-far-sum>]
  Both resulting sums over dyadic $M$ tend to zero with $L$. Explicitly, for every $0 < eta < 2$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.22)")
   $ sum_(M in { 1 \, 2 \, 4 \, dots.h }) (frac(M, L + M))^2 M^(- eta) lt.eq C_eta L^(- eta) . $ <eq:charge-dyadic-sum>]
  For $M lt.eq L$ this follows by summing $L^(- 2) M^(2 - eta)$; for $M > L$ it follows by summing $M^(- eta)$.
    Apply this with $eta = 1 \/ 16$ and $eta = 1 \/ 24$ to (6.19) and (6.21).
    The node summation rule after (6.15) now gives an error $o_L \( 1 \)
    S_(L \, 3 \/ 2)$ for the entire centered E contribution.
  There is no uncompensated electron scalar in this argument.
    Every F flag and every terminal flag contributes $- \( 1 \/ 3 - o_L \( 1 \) \) f \( k \)$,
    while all E contributions and all remainders cost $o_L \( 1 \) S_(L \, 3 \/ 2)$.
    Choose a fixed $L = L_1$, after $L_0$ and the constant in Corollary 32,
    so that the combined error is at most $1 \/ 6$. This proves (6.2) with $c = 1 \/ 6$. ◻
]

=== 6.4 Rotation averaging and local interactions <rotation-averaging-and-local-interactions>

The summability of $f_(L_1 \, 3 \/ 2)$ removes the power of the area in Corollary 32.
  We state the resulting estimate in the form needed for the energy argument.

#thm([Theorem 6.6.])[
  (Local expectations are linear in the number of holes).  #emph[Let $q =
    3 \( N - 1 \)$ and let $W$ be a neutral Hermitian interaction whose center density and rapid locality bounds
    are uniform in $q$. For every unit vector $psi in Z_(n \, q)$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.23)")
 $ \| chevron.l W chevron.r_psi - chevron.l W chevron.r_(upright("Laughlin") \, N) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|]
   lt.eq C_W \( N - n \) . $ <eq:charge-local-linear>]

 For interactions supported in radius $r$ with local norm at most one,
   $C_W$ is bounded by $C \( 1 + r \)^B$ for a fixed exponent $B$.
   The same conclusion holds for zero-mode density matrices by linearity, with the expected value of $N -
   cal(N)$ on the right.]
] <thm:charge-local-linear>

#proof[
  Use the local readout estimate with $a = 7 \/ 4$, now comparing $W_0$ to $S_(L_1 \, 3 \/ 2)$.
    Since the flux is divisible by three, the reference is the filled Laughlin expectation.
    The rotation average (6.17) and the second part of Proposition 28 give
  $ integral_(S U \( 2 \)) chevron.l S_(L_1 \, 3 \/ 2)^g chevron.r_psi thin d g lt.eq
    frac(h, 3 c \( q + 1 \)) sum_(j = 0)^q f_(L_1 \, 3 \/ 2) \( j \) lt.eq frac(C h, q + 1) \, $
  because $L_1$ is fixed and $sum_(j gt.eq 0) f_(L_1 \, 3 \/ 2) \( j \) < oo$.
    Integration over centers costs area $O \( q + 1 \)$, exactly as in the proof of Corollary 32.
    The result is $C_W h = 3 C_W \( N - n \)$; absorb the factor three into $C_W$.
    Polynomial radius costs and rapid shell summability are preserved by that argument.
    Decomposing a density matrix into number sectors and then into pure states proves the last assertion. ◻
]

== 7 Local removal of interaction energy <sec:energy>

The estimate on zero modes controls the cost of a missing electron.
  We now connect an arbitrary state to the zero space by a local process that removes particles.
  Its interaction energy decays exponentially, and its expected particle loss is bounded by its initial
  interaction energy. In particular, adding electrons above Laughlin filling has a uniform energy cost.

Write $B_p = B \( v_p \)$, $0 lt.eq p lt.eq 2 q - 2$, for the normalized pair annihilators of (2.5),
  and put $d_a = 2 q - 1$. For a rotation $g in S U \( 2 \)$,
  $A \( g \)$ denotes the conjugate of an operator $A$ by the Fock representation.
  Haar measure $d g$ has mass one. The rotation average of any normalized pair row resolves the interaction:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.1)")
 $ d_a integral B_p \( g \)^(*) B_p \( g \) thin d g = H_q . $ <eq:pair-resolution>]

This is Schur’s lemma on the spin-$\( q - 1 \)$ pair space $V_q$.

#thm([Theorem 7.1.])[
  (Local energy descent).  #emph[There are constants $c > 0$, $L < oo$, and $q_0$ such that, for every $q gt.eq
    q_0$, there is a measurable family of rapidly local physical operators $J_y$ on $cal(F)_q$ satisfying
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.2)")
 $ integral J_y^(*) J_y thin d nu_q \( y \) = H_q \, wide integral J_y^(*) H_q J_y thin d nu_q \( y \) lt.eq
   H_q^2 - c H_q . $ <eq:energy-descent>]

 Every $J_y$ has a definite particle loss $r_y in { 2 \, dots.h \, L }$, so that $\[ cal(N) \, J_y \] = -
   r_y J_y$. The center measure has bounded mass per unit ball,
   and all rapid-locality bounds are independent of $q$ and of the particle sector.]
] <thm:local-energy-descent>

The first operation in the construction is $B_0 \( g \)$,
  which removes a pair near the point $g dot.op upright("north")$.
  We then partially empty a fixed neighborhood of that point.
  This second operation suppresses the nearby pair correlations left behind by the first removal.
  We first identify the part of $H_q^2$ that pays for the surviving correlations.

=== 7.1 The four-body term available for descent <the-four-body-term-available-for-descent>

The maps $cal(L)_k$ and $W_4$ are defined in (2.4) and (2.7).
  Thus $cal(L)_4 \( \| u chevron.r chevron.l v \| \) = B \( u \)^(*) B \( v \)$ and $W_4 \( v ⊗ w \) = v and w$.
  Let $P_(q \, ell)$ be the projection in $V_q ⊗ V_q$ onto total spin $2 \( q - 1 \) - ell$, for $0 lt.eq
  ell lt.eq 2 q - 2$, and set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.3)")
 $ R_q^(upright("hi")) = sum_(ell = 23)^(2 q - 2) P_(q \, ell) \, wide R_q^(upright("lo")) = I -
   R_q^(upright("hi")) . $ <eq:high-pair-blocks>]

For large $q$, $R_q^(upright("lo"))$ contains precisely the small deficits $ell = 0 \, dots.h \, 22$.
  Proposition 39, proved in the appendix, gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.4)")
 $ H_q^2 gt.eq c_0 H_q + cal(L)_4 \( W_4 R_q^(upright("hi")) W_4^(*) \) wide \( 0 < c_0 < 1 \/ 25 \)
   $ <eq:retained-energy>]

for all sufficiently large $q$, uniformly on $cal(F)_q$.

This statement contains more information than the unperturbed spectral gap.
  The normal-ordering identity for $H_q^2$ contains the full four-body term $cal(L)_4 \( W_4 W_4^(*) \)$.
  The finite comparison of [16] Sections 2–6 uses only the blocks $ell lt.eq 22$.
  The appendix includes the finite certificate and its transfer to finite $q$,
  and keeps the other blocks with coefficient one. The loss construction will use precisely this part of the square.

=== 7.2 Emptying a neighborhood without increasing distant pair energy <emptying-a-neighborhood-without-increasing-distant-pair-energy>

Choose a smooth nondecreasing $T : bb(R) arrow.r \[ 0 \, 1 \]$ such that $T = 0$ on $\( - oo \, 1 \]$ and $T =
  1$ on $\[ 3 \, oo \)$. We choose it flat at the two endpoints and, in addition, require

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.5)")
 $ \| T^(\( r \)) \( x \) \| lt.eq C_r sqrt(1 - T \( x \)) wide \( r gt.eq 1 \) . $ <eq:evacuation-cutoff>]

For example, with $eta \( x \) = e^(- 1 \/ x)$ for $x > 0$ and $eta \( x \) = 0$ for $x lt.eq 0$,
  one may take $T \( x \) = eta \( x - 1 \) \/ \( eta \( x - 1 \) + eta \( 3 - x \) \)$.
  The estimate follows by differentiating near $x = 3$; an exponential $e^(- 1 \/ \( 3 - x \))$ times any fixed
  negative power of $3 - x$ is bounded by a constant times $e^(- 1 \/ \( 2 \( 3 - x \) \))$.
  Away from that endpoint the estimate is immediate from smoothness.

For an integer $M gt.eq 2$, put $t_j = T \( j \/ M \)$ and $n_j = c_j^(*) c_j$. The two operators

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.6)")
 $ K_(j \, 0) = I - n_j + t_j n_j \, wide K_(j \, 1) = sqrt(1 - t_j^2) thin c_j $ <eq:single-mode-loss>]

define a trace-preserving channel, since $K_(j \, 0)^(*) K_(j \, 0) + K_(j \, 1)^(*) K_(j \, 1) = I$.
  Compose these channels in increasing order of $j$, for $0 lt.eq j < 3 M$.
  Write $K_alpha$ for the resulting products of Kraus operators, where $alpha in { 0 \, 1 }^(3 M)$,
  and write $cal(E)_M$ for the channel. Its dual on observables is $cal(E)_M^(*) \( O \) =
  sum_alpha K_alpha^(*) O K_alpha$.

The canonical anticommutation relations imply a useful exact formula.
  On an even normal-ordered monomial, the dual of the $j$th channel multiplies by $t_j$ for each occurrence of
  $c_j$ or $c_j^(*)$. Indeed, if neither occurs, the even monomial commutes with both operators of mode $j$ and
  completeness gives the claim. If exactly one occurs, the loss contribution vanishes and the no-loss contribution
  gives $t_j$. If both occur, writing them as an occupation factor gives $t_j^2$. Consequently

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.7)")
 $ cal(E)_M^(*) \( H_q \) = sum_(b = 1)^(2 q - 1) tilde(B)_(b - 1)^(*) tilde(B)_(b - 1) \, wide tilde(B)_(b - 1) =
   sum_(i < j\
i + j = b) t_i t_j thin overline(v_(b - 1) \( i \, j \)) thin c_j c_i . $ <eq:evacuated-interaction>]

Here and below terms with an orbital index outside ${ 0 \, dots.h \, q }$ are absent.

#thm([Lemma 7.2.])[
  (Pair energy after local loss).  #emph[There is a sequence $epsilon_M arrow.r 0$ such that, for every $M gt.eq
    2$ and every $q gt.eq 30 M$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.8)")
 $ sum_(b = 1)^(2 q - 1) tilde(B)_(b - 1)^(*) tilde(B)_(b - 1) lt.eq sum_(b > M) B_(b - 1)^(*) B_(b - 1) +
   epsilon_M I quad upright("on ") cal(F)_q . $ <eq:evacuation-rows>]

]
] <lem:evacuation-rows>

#proof[
  The following two elementary estimates specialize the general pair localization bounds to the cutoff used here.
    In particular, the exponential estimate on distant rows will let us preserve coefficient one on their energy.
    Both follow directly from the coefficients in (2.5). For $b lt.eq q \/ 2$ and every fixed integer $r gt.eq 0$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.9)")
   $ sum_(i < j\
i + j = b) \| v_(b - 1) \( i \, j \) \| thin \| i - b \/ 2 \|^r lt.eq
  C_r b^(r \/ 2 + 1 \/ 4) . $ <eq:pair-absolute-moments>]
  For $b > 10 M$ and $q gt.eq 30 M$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.10)")
   $ sum_(i < j \, med i + j = b\
i < 3 M) \| v_(b - 1) \( i \, j \) \| lt.eq C b^(3 \/ 2) e^(- c_1 b) . $ <eq:pair-remote-tail>]
  Constants may change after decreasing $c_1 > 0$. Here is a direct verification. Set
  $ p_(q \, b) \( i \) = frac(binom(q, i) binom(q, b - i), binom(2 q, b)) . $
  The exact pair formula gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.11)")
   $ \| v_(b - 1) \( i \, b - i \) \|^2 = frac(2 \( 2 q - 1 \) \( 2 i - b \)^2, b \( 2 q - b \))
     p_(q \, b) \( i \) \, wide frac(p_(q \, b) \( i + 1 \), p_(q \, b) \( i \)) =
     frac(\( q - i \) \( b - i \), \( i + 1 \) \( q - b + i + 1 \)) . $ <eq:pair-hypergeometric>]
  When $b lt.eq q \/ 2$, these ratios, their inverses, and symmetry about $b \/ 2$ show
  $ p_(q \, b) \( i \) lt.eq C b^(- 1 \/ 2) exp { - c_2 \( i - b \/ 2 \)^2 \/ b } . $
  For completeness, on the upper half $i gt.eq \( b - 1 \) \/ 2$,
    the first factor in the ratio is at most one and the second is at most $exp
    { - \( 2 i + 1 - b \) \/ \( b + 1 \) }$. Summing logarithms from a middle index gives the Gaussian upper bound
    relative to the middle value. In the range $\| i - b \/ 2 \| lt.eq sqrt(b)$,
    the absolute value of that logarithm is at most $C \( 1 + \| i - b \/ 2 \| \) \/ b$.
    Summing these lower bounds and using $sum_i p_(q \, b) \( i \) = 1$ bounds the middle value by $C \/ sqrt(b)$.
    Insertion in (7.11), followed by summing Gaussian moments, proves (7.9).
  To prove (7.10), there is nothing to check if $b > q + 3 M$, because the sum is empty.
    Otherwise $b lt.eq q + 3 M lt.eq 11 q \/ 10$, so $2 \( 2 q - 1 \) \/ \[ b \( 2 q - b \) \] lt.eq
    C \/ b$ in (7.11). For every allowed $i lt.eq 2 b \/ 5$ and $b gt.eq 20$,
    the ratio in (7.11) is at least $4 \/ 3$. Starting with $i < 3 M < 3 b \/ 10$ and taking at least $b \/ 10 -
    O \( 1 \)$ steps toward $2 b \/ 5$ gives $p_(q \, b) \( i \) lt.eq C e^(- c b)$,
    since each probability is at most one. The square root of (7.11) is therefore at most $C sqrt(b) thin
    e^(- c_1 b)$, and there are at most $b$ terms. This proves (7.10).
  The modified row vanishes when $b lt.eq M$. For $M < b lt.eq 10 M$, put $x = b \/ \( 2 M \)$ and $t =
    T \( x \)$. The smooth even function $z mapsto T \( x + z \) T \( x - z \)$ has zero first derivative at zero.
    By (7.5), every derivative of positive order there is bounded by $C_r sqrt(1 - t)$.
    Taylor expansion through degree $41$, with a bounded remainder of order $42$, and (7.9) yield
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.12)")
   $ tilde(B)_(b - 1) = t^2 B_(b - 1) + E_b \, wide parallel E_b parallel lt.eq C M^(- 3 \/ 4) sqrt(1 - t) +
     C M^(- 20) . $ <eq:weighted-row-error>]
  We used $parallel c_j c_i parallel lt.eq 1$. More explicitly, the degree-$r$ contribution is bounded by
  $ C_r M^(- r) b^(r \/ 2 + 1 \/ 4) sqrt(1 - t) \, wide 2 lt.eq r lt.eq 41 \, $
  and the remainder is at most $C M^(- 42) b^(21 + 1 \/ 4)$.
  Let $delta = M^(- 8)$ and $s = 1 - t + delta$. For any vector $phi.alt$, Cauchy’s inequality gives
  $ parallel \( t^2 B_(b - 1) + E_b \) phi.alt parallel^2 lt.eq \( t^4 + s \) parallel B_(b - 1) phi.alt
    parallel^2 + \( 1 + t^4 \/ s \) parallel E_b phi.alt parallel^2 . $
  Since $t^4 + s lt.eq 1 + delta$, (7.12) bounds the second term by $C M^(- 3 \/ 2) parallel phi.alt parallel^2$.
    Summing over the $O \( M \)$ central rows costs $C M^(- 1 \/ 2) parallel phi.alt parallel^2$.
    The additional coefficient $delta$ also disappears in this error:
    (7.9) with $r = 0$ implies $sum_(M < b lt.eq 10 M) parallel B_(b - 1) parallel^2 lt.eq C M^(3 \/ 2)$.
  Finally, for $b > 10 M$ the coefficients of $tilde(B)_(b - 1) -
    B_(b - 1)$ are supported where $i < 3 M$ or $j < 3 M$. The increasing ordering reduces this to $i < 3 M$.
    Equation (7.10) bounds this difference in operator norm by $C b^(3 \/ 2) e^(- c_1 b)$,
    while normalization of the pair vector gives $parallel B_(b - 1) parallel lt.eq sqrt(b)$.
    The norm of the difference of the two row squares is therefore summable over $b > 10 M$,
    with sum at most $C e^(- c_3 M)$. Combining the three ranges proves the assertion, with $epsilon_M lt.eq
    C \( M^(- 1 \/ 2) + M^(- 13 \/ 2) + e^(- c_3 M) \)$. ◻
]

=== 7.3 Rotation averaging suppresses the small-deficit blocks <rotation-averaging-suppresses-the-small-deficit-blocks>

Retaining only the rows whose index sum exceeds $M$ gives a positive operator on the two pair factors:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.13)")
 $ A_(q \, M) = d_a integral \| v_0 \( g \) chevron.r chevron.l v_0 \( g \) \| ⊗
   (sum_(k + 1 > M) \| v_k \( g \) chevron.r chevron.l v_k \( g \) \|) thin d g . $ <eq:pair-tail-average>]

If the sum includes all $k$, the operator equals $I$ by Schur’s lemma. Consequently $0 lt.eq A_(q \, M) lt.eq I$.
  This bound handles every retained block with the same coefficient one as in (7.4).
  It remains to make the coefficients on the finitely many unretained blocks small and bound their lifts by $H_q$.

#thm([Lemma 7.3.])[
  (Vanishing weight on fixed deficits).  #emph[For $A_(q \, M)$ in (7.13), there are coefficients $0 lt.eq
    a_(q \, M \, ell) lt.eq 1$ such that
$ A_(q \, M) = sum_(ell = 0)^(2 q - 2) a_(q \, M \, ell) P_(q \, ell) . $

 For every fixed $ell gt.eq 0$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.14)")
 $ lim_(M arrow.r oo) limsup_(q arrow.r oo) a_(q \, M \, ell) = 0 . $ <eq:spin-tail-limit>]

 Moreover, for each fixed $ell$ there is $C_ell < oo$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.15)")
 $ cal(L)_4 \( W_4 P_(q \, ell) W_4^(*) \) lt.eq C_ell H_q $ <eq:fixed-block-energy>]

 for all sufficiently large $q$.]
] <lem:pair-spin-tail>

#proof[
  The tensor product of two spin-$\( q - 1 \)$ representations is multiplicity free.
    Hence rotation invariance and $0 lt.eq A_(q \, M) lt.eq I$ prove the first assertion.
    Put $a = q - 1$ and $d_(q \, ell) = 4 q - 3 - 2 ell$. If $C_q \( ell \, k \)$ is the coupling coefficient of
    $v_0 ⊗ v_k$ in total spin $2 a - ell$ at total lowering degree $k$, then
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.16)")
   $ a_(q \, M \, ell) = frac(2 q - 1, d_(q \, ell)) sum_(k + 1 > M) \| C_q \( ell \, k \) \|^2 .
     $ <eq:spin-tail-coefficient>]
  The unrestricted sum, including all $k$, has coefficient exactly one.
  We give the fixed-$k$ limit, including its normalization.
    At a fixed total degree, the two spin lowering operators, divided by $sqrt(2 a)$,
    converge to multiplication by two independent oscillator variables $X \, Y$.
    The highest vector of deficit $ell$ converges to $\( X - Y \)^ell \/ sqrt(2^ell ell !)$:
    applying the raising operator gives successive coefficient ratio $-
    sqrt(\( ell - p \) \/ \( p + 1 \))$ in the limit, which fixes this normalized vector.
    Normalized subsequent lowering multiplies by $\( X + Y \) \/ sqrt(2 \( k - ell \))$ at degree $k$.
    Thus the degree-$k$ coupled vector converges, in the orthonormal monomial basis, to
  $ frac(\( X - Y \)^ell \( X + Y \)^(k - ell), 2^(k \/ 2) sqrt(ell ! \( k - ell \) !)) . $
  Its coefficient of $Y^k \/ sqrt(k !)$ has squared modulus $2^(- k) binom(k, ell)$, and hence
  $ \| C_q \( ell \, k \) \|^2 arrow.r 2^(- k) binom(k, ell) . $
  There are only finitely many degrees below a fixed $M$. Subtracting these from the exact unrestricted
    coefficient one gives
  $ lim_(q arrow.r oo) a_(q \, M \, ell) = 1 - 1 / 2 sum_(ell lt.eq k lt.eq M - 1) 2^(- k) binom(k, ell) . $
  The identity $sum_(k = ell)^oo 2^(- k) binom(k, ell) = 2$ proves (7.14).
    This finite-head argument requires no uniform convergence over an unbounded set of coupling coefficients.
  For (7.15), choose a normalized highest vector $z_(q \, ell) = sum_(p = 0)^ell s_p v_p ⊗ v_(ell - p)$,
    with $sum_p \| s_p \|^2 = 1$. Its wedge annihilator is $sum_p overline(s_p) B_(ell - p) B_p$.
    Every $B_(ell - p)$ uses only the first $ell + 2$ orbitals and has norm at most $sqrt(ell + 2)$. Therefore
  $ parallel B \( W_4 z_(q \, ell) \) psi parallel^2 lt.eq \( ell + 2 \) sum_(p = 0)^ell parallel B_p psi parallel^2 . $
  Resolve $P_(q \, ell)$ by the orbit of its highest vector and use (7.1). This yields
  $ cal(L)_4 \( W_4 P_(q \, ell) W_4^(*) \) lt.eq frac(d_(q \, ell), 2 q - 1) \( ell + 2 \) \( ell + 1 \) H_q \, $
  whose coefficient is bounded for fixed $ell$. ◻
]

#proof[
  For the moment fix $M$, and define
  $ J_(g \, alpha) = K_alpha \( g \) B_0 \( g \) \, wide d nu_q \( g \, alpha \) = \( 2 q - 1 \) thin d g $
  with counting measure in $alpha$. Completeness of the loss channel and (7.1) give $integral J_y^(*) J_y thin d
    nu_q = H_q$. Lemma 35, applied to $B_0 \( g \) psi$ and then averaged, gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.17)")
   $ integral J_y^(*) H_q J_y thin d nu_q \( y \) & = d_a integral B_0 \( g \)^(*) cal(E)_(M \, g)^(*) \( H_q \)
     B_0 \( g \) thin d g\
 & lt.eq cal(L)_4 \( W_4 A_(q \, M) W_4^(*) \) + epsilon_M H_q . $ <eq:loss-four-body>]
  The equality between the averaged pair products and the lifted four-body operator uses ordered tensor factors.
    There is no factor of two: $B \( v and w \) = B \( w \) B \( v \)$ and the lift sums each ordered pair once.
  By Lemma 36 and positivity of the lift,
  $ cal(L)_4 \( W_4 A_(q \, M) W_4^(*) \) lt.eq cal(L)_4 \( W_4 R_q^(upright("hi")) W_4^(*) \) +
    (sum_(ell = 0)^22 C_ell a_(q \, M \, ell)) H_q . $
  Fix $c_0 = 1 \/ 50$ in (7.4). Choose $M$ large enough, and then $q$ large enough, that $epsilon_M +
    sum_(ell = 0)^22 C_ell a_(q \, M \, ell) lt.eq c_0 \/ 2$.
    Equations (7.4) and (7.17) prove (7.2) with $c = c_0 \/ 2$.
  Each $K_alpha$ is a product of $3 M$ operators of particle loss zero or one,
    so $J_(g \, alpha)$ has loss between $2$ and $L = 3 M + 2$.
    All modes involved in $J_(g \, alpha)$ have index below $3 M$, apart from identity factors.
    To see operator-norm locality directly, use the frame isometry $sans(S)_q : U_q arrow.r
    ell^2 \( Lambda_q \)$ and write $c \( f \) = B \( f \)$ on the slot Fock space.
    If $P_R$ cuts off slots outside the radius-$R$ ball around $g dot.op upright("north")$,
    the canonical anticommutation relations give
  $ parallel c \( sans(S)_q e_j \( g \) \) - c \( P_R sans(S)_q e_j \( g \) \) parallel =
    parallel \( I - P_R \) sans(S)_q e_j \( g \) parallel_2 lt.eq C_(D \, M) R^(- D) . $
  The last estimate follows from Lemma 4 and the slot volume bound,
    with a larger decay order before summing squares. The same estimate for a product follows by telescoping its
    finitely many factors, or by Lemma 7. Thus the jumps are rapidly local around $g dot.op upright("north")$,
    uniformly in $q$. There are only $2^(3 M)$ auxiliary labels, with $M$ now fixed.
    The pushforward of $\( 2 q - 1 \) d g$ to the physical sphere has area density
    $\( 2 q - 1 \) \/ \( 2 pi q \)$; the remaining rotation angle has mass one.
    Including all auxiliary labels therefore gives total center density $2^(3 M) \( 2 q - 1 \) \/ \( 2 pi q \)$,
    uniformly bounded in $q$ for this fixed $M$. These observations prove all locality and density assertions. ◻
]

=== 7.4 Particle loss, excess charge, and a coercive Fock Hamiltonian <particle-loss-excess-charge-and-a-coercive-fock-hamiltonian>

Let $q = 3 \( N - 1 \)$ and let $rho_t$ evolve by the trace-preserving equation whose dual generator is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.18)")
 $ cal(D)_q \( O \) = integral J_y^(*) O J_y thin d nu_q \( y \) - 1 / 2 { H_q \, O } . $ <eq:loss-generator>]

This is a finite-dimensional Lindblad generator [10]. Positivity and trace preservation also follow directly by
  iterating variation of constants, with the completely positive gain map $rho mapsto integral J_y rho J_y^(*)
  thin d nu_q \( y \)$ and the no-jump map $rho mapsto e^(- t H_q \/ 2) rho e^(- t H_q \/ 2)$.
  We write $chevron.l O chevron.r_t = "Tr" \( rho_t O \)$.

#thm([Proposition 7.4.])[
  (Control of particle number).  #emph[There are constants $C_(upright("num")) < oo$, $mu > 0$, $a > 0$,
    and $C_G < oo$, independent of sufficiently large $q = 3 \( N - 1 \)$, such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.19)")
 $ cal(N) - N lt.eq C_(upright("num")) H_q . $ <eq:excess-number-bound>]

 The operator
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.20)")
 $ G_q = H_q + mu \( N - cal(N) \) $ <eq:coercive-G>]

 satisfies
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.21)")
 $ G_q gt.eq a #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] H_q + \| N - cal(N) \| #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] gt.eq
   g #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] I - P_(upright(L) \, N) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $ <eq:G-coercivity>]

 for a constant $g > 0$, with $P_(upright(L) \, N)$ extended by zero on the other Fock sectors.
   For every initial density matrix, the evolution (7.18) obeys
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.22)")
 $ chevron.l H_q chevron.r_t & lt.eq e^(- c t) chevron.l H_q chevron.r_0 \,\
0 lt.eq chevron.l cal(N) chevron.r_0 - chevron.l cal(N) chevron.r_t & lt.eq
  C_(upright("num")) chevron.l H_q chevron.r_0 \,\
chevron.l G_q chevron.r_t & lt.eq C_G chevron.l G_q chevron.r_0 . $ <eq:loss-energy-decay>]

 Every limit point $rho_oo$ is supported on $ker H_q$, and
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.23)")
 $ 0 lt.eq "Tr" rho_oo \( N - cal(N) \) lt.eq C_G mu^(- 1) chevron.l G_q chevron.r_0 . $ <eq:loss-hole-control>]

]
] <prop:grand-canonical-coercivity>

#proof[
  Theorem 34 gives $cal(D)_q \( H_q \) lt.eq - c H_q$, proving (7.22). Since $\[ cal(N) \, J_y \] = - r_y J_y$,
  $ - cal(D)_q \( cal(N) \) = integral r_y J_y^(*) J_y thin d nu_q \( y \) \, wide 2 H_q lt.eq -
    cal(D)_q \( cal(N) \) lt.eq L H_q . $
  Integration proves (7.22) with $C_(upright("num")) = L \/ c$.
    Compactness of the density matrices in each fixed finite-dimensional Fock space gives limit points as $t
    arrow.r oo$. By energy decay each is supported on $ker H_q$.
    Lemma 2 shows that this kernel contains no sector of particle number exceeding $N$.
    Applying (7.22) to a state of definite particle number $n$ and passing to a limit point gives $n - N lt.eq
    C_(upright("num")) chevron.l H_q chevron.r_0$. This proves (7.19) on each sector, and hence on Fock space.
  Choose $0 < mu lt.eq min \( 1 \, \( 2 C_(upright("num")) \)^(- 1) \)$.
    In sectors $n lt.eq N$, $G_q = H_q + mu \( N - n \)$ dominates $mu \( H_q + N - n \)$.
    In sectors $n > N$, (7.19) gives $G_q gt.eq H_q \/ 2$ and $H_q + n -
    N lt.eq \( 1 + C_(upright("num")) \) H_q$. This proves the first inequality in (7.21) with $a =
    min \( mu \, \[ 2 \( 1 + C_(upright("num")) \) \]^(- 1) \)$.
    For $n eq.not N$, $\| N - n \| gt.eq 1$; for $n = N$, the uniform gap and uniqueness in Theorem 3 give
    $H_(N \, q) gt.eq \( 1 \/ 25 \) \( I - P_(upright(L) \, N) \)$. Thus the second inequality holds with $g = a \/ 25$.
  Finally, energy decay and number control give
  $ chevron.l G_q chevron.r_t lt.eq chevron.l G_q chevron.r_0 +
    mu C_(upright("num")) chevron.l H_q chevron.r_0 lt.eq \( 1 + mu C_(upright("num")) \/ a \) chevron.l G_q
    chevron.r_0 . $
  Take $C_G = 1 + mu C_(upright("num")) \/ a$. Passing to a limit point, where $H_q = 0$, proves (7.23). ◻
]

== 8 Relative bounds and stability <sec:stability>

The preceding sections provide two estimates with different roles.
  The zero-mode bound controls a local observable after particles have been removed.
  The loss evolution connects an arbitrary state to that zero space at a cost measured by its initial interaction
  energy. We first combine them into a relative form bound.
  Spectral transport will then put the disorder perturbation in the class covered by that bound.

Throughout this section, $q = 3 \( N - 1 \)$ is sufficiently large,
  $Omega in Z_(N \, q)$ is the unit Laughlin vector, and $H = H_q$.
  Choose the fixed $mu > 0$ from Proposition 37 and write

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.1)")
 $ G = H + mu \( N - cal(N) \) . $ <eq:stability-G>]

Thus $G$ is positive, has kernel $bb(C) Omega$, and has a uniform gap.
  Moreover it controls both $H$ and $\| N - cal(N) \|$.

=== 8.1 A relative bound for terms that annihilate the ground state <a-relative-bound-for-terms-that-annihilate-the-ground-state>

We use the local norm $parallel A parallel_(x \, ell)$ of (3.10) at one fixed order $ell$.
  Partition the sphere into cells of uniformly bounded diameter whose centers satisfy the two-dimensional volume
  bound of Section 3. For a continuous interaction, collect all terms centered in a cell and assign their integral
  to that cell’s center. Moving a center a bounded distance changes the local norm by a fixed factor.
  We normalize the total localization norm in each cell to at most one.

Physical operators act trivially on the complementary slot modes;
  the inequalities below are on physical Fock space. Averaging ball approximants over total-slot-number rotations
  preserves support and approximation error, so approximants of a neutral operator may be chosen neutral.
  Taking Hermitian parts gives Hermitian approximants.

Choose $ell$ larger than the polynomial support-radius exponent in Theorem 33,
  and larger than any fixed geometric exponents needed below.
  A dyadic decomposition into ball approximants shows that the zero-mode estimate extends to interactions with
  bounded total $parallel dot.op parallel_(x \, ell)$ per cell.
  Explicitly, the shell of radius $2^k$ has norm $O \( 2^(- k ell) \)$,
  while its zero-mode cost is $O \( 2^(k B) \)$ for a fixed $B < ell$; the series converges.

#thm([Proposition 8.1.])[
  (Relative form bound).  #emph[For this fixed sufficiently large $ell$,
    there is a constant $C$ independent of $q$ such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.2)")
 $ - C G med lt.eq med sum_x A_x med lt.eq med C G $ <eq:relative-bound>]

 whenever the $A_x$ are physical, neutral, Hermitian operators satisfying $A_x Omega =
   0$ and $parallel A_x parallel_(x \, ell) lt.eq 1$. If the sum or integral of localization norms in each cell is
   at most $S$, the conclusion becomes $- C S G lt.eq sum_x A_x lt.eq C S G$.]
] <prop:relative-bound>

#proof[
  Fix $q$ for the moment and let $K = K_q$ be the least constant in (8.2) for the stated class. It is finite:
    every admissible sum annihilates $Omega$ on both sides, its norm is bounded by the finite number of cells,
    and $G$ has a positive gap on $Omega^perp$. This observation gives no useful uniform bound;
    we obtain one from the loss evolution.
  Let $J_y$ be the jumps of Theorem 34, including their bounded auxiliary indices in the measure $d y$. They obey
  $ integral J_y^(*) J_y thin d y = H \, wide J_y Omega = 0 quad upright("for almost every ") y . $
  The second assertion follows by taking the expectation of the first in $Omega$.
    Put $C_(x y) = \[ A_x \, J_y \]$ and $D_(x y) = 1 + d \( x \, y \)$.
    Since $A_x$ is even, the ordinary commutator vanishes for disjointly supported approximants,
    including when $J_y$ is odd. Lemma 7(iii) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.3)")
   $ parallel C_(x y) parallel lt.eq C D_(x y)^(- ell) \, wide parallel C_(x y) parallel_(x \, ell) lt.eq C \,
     wide parallel C_(x y)^(*) C_(x y) parallel_(x \, ell) lt.eq C D_(x y)^(- ell) . $ <eq:commutator-norms>]
  The last estimate retains the same order $ell$ required of $A_x$.
    Thus, after integration in $y$, the positive operators $C_(x y)^(*)
    C_(x y)$ remain in the class whose best relative-bound constant is $K$.
    Fixing one polynomial norm makes this closure possible.
  Every $C_(x y)$ annihilates $Omega$, because both $A_x$ and $J_y$ do.
    Therefore $C_(x y)^(*) C_(x y)$ is a physical, positive, neutral,
    centered operator to which the definition of $K$ applies. Choose
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.4)")
   $ 2 < b < ell - 2 . $ <eq:distance-weight-choice>]
  The two-dimensional volume bound and (8.3) give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.5)")
   $ sum_x integral D_(x y)^b C_(x y)^(*) C_(x y) thin d y lt.eq C K G . $ <eq:squared-commutator-sum>]
  Indeed for each fixed $x$ the integrated operator has localization norm at most $C integral D_(x y)^(b - ell)
    thin d y lt.eq C$, and kills $Omega$. Collect it as a single admissible term at cell $x$.
    This also explains the continuous-family extension in the statement.
  Let $rho_t$ be the state evolution dual to the loss generator, starting at an arbitrary density matrix $rho_0$.
    All observables in the desired inequality preserve number,
    so replacing $rho_0$ by its number-diagonal part changes none of the relevant expectations.
    For $A = sum_x A_x$, the Lindblad identity reads
  $ frac(d, d t) "Tr" \( rho_t A \) = "Re" sum_x integral "Tr" \( rho_t J_y^(*) C_(x y) \) thin d y . $
  Apply Cauchy–Schwarz to this sum with weights $D_(x y)^(- b)$ and $D_(x y)^b$.
    Since $sum_x D_(x y)^(- b) lt.eq C$ uniformly in $y$, (8.5) yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.6)")
   $ lr(|frac(d, d t) "Tr" \( rho_t A \)|) lt.eq C #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Tr" \( rho_t H \)
     #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(1 \/ 2) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] K "Tr" \( rho_t G \)
     #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(1 \/ 2) . $ <eq:relative-derivative>]
  The energy-descent estimates give constants $c \, C > 0$ such that
  $ "Tr" \( rho_t H \) lt.eq e^(- c t) "Tr" \( rho_0 H \) \, wide "Tr" \( rho_t G \) lt.eq C "Tr" \( rho_0 G \) \,
    wide H lt.eq C G . $
  Integrating (8.6) therefore costs at most $C sqrt(K) "Tr" \( rho_0 G \)$.
  Along a sequence $t_j arrow.r oo$, finite dimensionality gives a limit state $rho_oo$ supported on $Z_q$.
    Its sectors have $n lt.eq N$ by Lemma 2. The zero-mode bound and $chevron.l Omega \, A Omega chevron.r =
    0$ show that
  $ \| "Tr" \( rho_oo A \) \| lt.eq C "Tr" \( rho_oo \( N - cal(N) \) \) = C mu^(- 1) "Tr" \( rho_oo G \) lt.eq
    C "Tr" \( rho_0 G \) . $
  Combining the two bounds, and then taking the supremum over all admissible interactions and states, gives
  $ K lt.eq C + C sqrt(K) . $
  Solving this quadratic inequality for $sqrt(K)$ proves a bound independent of $q$, as required. ◻
]

=== 8.2 Transporting the ground state <transporting-the-ground-state>

The perturbation is not termwise centered in the sense of Proposition 38.
  Spectral transport produces that property. We give the argument explicitly because it must follow the perturbed
  ground line rather than keep the original line fixed. The smooth frequency filter implements the quasiadiabatic
  idea of [6] in the exact spectral-flow form of [2]; the locality estimates needed here were established in Section 3.

Let $phi$ be real and measurable, $parallel phi parallel_oo lt.eq 1$. On Fock space set

$ V = d Gamma \( T_q \( phi \) \) \, wide H_s = H + s V \, wide - 1 lt.eq s lt.eq 1 . $

The coherent resolution (2.3) writes $V$ as a bounded-density integral of neutral Hermitian terms

$ V_x = kappa_q phi \( x \) c \( k_x \)^(*) c \( k_x \) . $

They are rapidly local with uniform bounds by Lemma 4. The same holds for $H$, using the rotations of $v_0 =
  e_0 and e_1$:

$ H = \( 2 q - 1 \) integral_(S U \( 2 \)) B \( v_0 \( g \) \)^(*) B \( v_0 \( g \) \) thin d g . $

The measure has uniformly bounded density on the physical sphere.
  In particular every $H_s$ has uniform interaction bounds for $\| s \| lt.eq 1$,
  even though $parallel V parallel$ can grow with $N$.

Fix $g_0 = 1 \/ 25$ and work initially on a connected parameter interval containing zero where the $N$-sector
  ground state is simple and the gap above it exceeds $g_0 \/ 2$.
  Such an interval exists at every fixed flux by finite-dimensional perturbation theory.
  Choose once and for all a smooth odd real function $F$ satisfying

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.7)")
 $ F \( omega \) = - 1 / omega quad \( \| omega \| gt.eq g_0 \/ 2 \) \, wide F \( omega \) =
   0 quad \( \| omega \| lt.eq g_0 \/ 4 \) . $ <eq:transport-filter>]

It can be chosen with an inverse Fourier kernel having every absolute polynomial moment.
  The $1 \/ omega$ tail is square-integrable and has integrable derivatives of all positive orders;
  near time zero Plancherel gives local integrability, and repeated integration by parts gives arbitrary decay for
  large time.

For each term of $V$, define $Y_x \( s \)$ by applying this frequency filter with respect to $H_s$.
  In an eigenbasis of $H_s$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.8)")
 $ chevron.l a \| Y_x \( s \) \| b chevron.r = F \( E_a - E_b \) chevron.l a \| V_x \| b chevron.r .
   $ <eq:transport-matrix-elements>]

Oddness of $F$ makes $Y_x$ anti-Hermitian. Every $Y_x$ is physical, neutral,
  and rapidly local with uniform bounds; put $Y \( s \) = integral Y_x \( s \) thin d A \( x \)$ and solve

$ U' \( s \) = Y \( s \) U \( s \) \, wide U \( 0 \) = I . $

This unitary preserves number. If $P_s$ is the $N$-sector ground projection,
  the usual spectral derivative formula and (8.7) give $P'_s = \[ Y \( s \) \, P_s \]$.
  Consequently $U \( s \) P_0 U \( s \)^(*) = P_s$. Neutrality is important here:
  the matrix elements crossing the ground projection stay in the $N$-particle sector.
  No assertion about a perturbed Fock-space gap is needed.

For each $x$ separately, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.9)")
 $ Q_x \( s \) & = U \( s \)^(*) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] V_x + \[ H_s \, Y_x \( s \) \]
   #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] U \( s \) \,\
a_x \( s \) & = chevron.l Omega \, Q_x \( s \) Omega chevron.r \, wide A_x \( s \) = Q_x \( s \) -
  a_x \( s \) I . $ <eq:transport-centered-terms>]

These are Hermitian neutral physical operators. For an excited $N$-sector eigenvector $a$ and the ground vector
  $0$, their unconjugated off-diagonal matrix element is

$ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 1 + \( E_a - E_0 \) F \( E_a - E_0 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] chevron.l a \|
  V_x \| 0 chevron.r = 0 . $

Hermiticity gives the reverse vanishing. Thus each $Q_x \( s \)$ preserves $bb(C) Omega$, and $A_x \( s \) Omega =
  0$ exactly. The commutator with $H_s$ and the finite-parameter conjugation by $U \( s \)$ preserve rapid
  locality, uniformly on the bootstrap interval. The scalar satisfies $\| a_x \( s \) \| lt.eq
  parallel Q_x \( s \) parallel$; it can be assigned to the same center.
  Hence the interaction $A_x \( s \)$ satisfies Proposition 38 with a uniform size bound.

Let $E_0 \( s \)$ be the $N$-sector ground energy. Differentiating the pulled-back Hamiltonian and taking its
  expectation in $Omega$ gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.10)")
 $ frac(d, d s) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] U \( s \)^(*) H_s U \( s \) -
   E_0 \( s \) I #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = integral A_x \( s \) thin d A \( x \) \, wide - C G lt.eq
   integral A_x \( s \) thin d A \( x \) lt.eq C G . $ <eq:transport-relative-derivative>]

All differentiation is finite-dimensional; the integral notation may equally be collected into cells.
  On the $N$-sector, $G = H$. Integrating in either parameter direction yields

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.11)")
 $ U \( s \)^(*) H_s U \( s \) - E_0 \( s \) I gt.eq \( 1 - C \| s \| \) H wide
   upright("on ") and.big^N U_q . $ <eq:final-form-bound>]

The kernel on this interval is $bb(C) Omega$, so the min–max principle gives a gap of at least
  $\( 1 - C \| s \| \) g_0$.

Choose $lambda_(*) > 0$ with $lambda_(*) lt.eq 1$ and $C lambda_(*) lt.eq 1 \/ 4$.
  The improved lower bound $3 g_0 \/ 4$ prevents the gap from reaching the bootstrap boundary $g_0 \/
  2$ anywhere in $\[ - lambda_(*) \, lambda_(*) \]$. Indeed a first boundary point would contradict continuity of
  the ordered eigenvalues and (8.11). The construction therefore extends over this entire interval at every
  sufficiently large flux. Taking $Delta_(*) = g_0 \/ 2$ proves Theorem 1.

== 9 Retaining the unused four-body blocks <app:retained-blocks>

The local loss construction requires a stronger operator inequality than the unperturbed gap:
  most of the normal-ordered four-body term must remain on the right-hand side.
  We give the full finite-dimensional calculation that establishes this strengthening.
  The seven auxiliary squares, their rational certificate, and the finite-flux transfer are adapted from [16]
  Sections 2–6 and 8. The proof below retains the four-body blocks that are discarded when only the gap is sought.

We use the lift and wedge maps of (2.4) and (2.7), with increasing orthonormal wedges of norm one.
  Thus $B \( v and w \) = B \( w \) B \( v \)$, and the domain of $W_4$ uses ordered pair factors.
  Write $a = q - 1$ and $H = H_q$; the conventions give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.1)")
 $ cal(L)_k \( \| v chevron.r chevron.l w \| \) & = B \( v \)^dagger B \( w \) \,\
B_p = B \( v_p \) \, wide H & = cal(L)_2 \( Pi_(V_q) \) = sum_(p = 0)^(2 q - 2) B_p^dagger B_p . $ <rb:eq:lift>]

The dagger in this appendix denotes the same adjoint as the star in the main text.

The tensor product $V_q ⊗ V_q$ contains one copy of each spin $2 q - 2 - l$, $0 lt.eq l lt.eq 2 q - 2$.
  Let $P_(q \, l)$ be its orthogonal spin projection and define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.2)")
 $ R_q^(upright("lo")) = sum_(l = 0)^22 P_(q \, l) \, wide R_q^(upright("hi")) = I -
   R_q^(upright("lo")) quad \( q gt.eq 24 \) . $ <rb:eq:retained-projections>]

The spin-block label used in the calculation below is $D = l + 1$;
  thus $R_q^(upright("lo"))$ consists exactly of $1 lt.eq D lt.eq 23$.

#thm([Proposition 9.1.])[
  (Retained four-body blocks).  #emph[For every fixed $0 < c_0 < 1 \/ 25$ there exists $q_0$ such that,
    for every integer $q gt.eq q_0$, the following inequality holds on the entire Fock space $cal(F)_q$:
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.3)")
 $ H_q^2 gt.eq c_0 H_q + cal(L)_4 #h(-0.16667em) (W_4 R_q^(upright("hi")) W_4^dagger) . $ <rb:eq:retained-blocks>]

 The threshold is independent of particle number.]
] <prop:retained-blocks>

We prove the proposition by comparing a positive rotational average of seven explicit squares with the
  normal-ordered terms of $H_q^2$. The comparison uses the target four-body blocks $1 lt.eq D lt.eq 23$ only.
  All other target blocks consequently remain with coefficient one.

#thm([Lemma 9.2.])[
  (Normal-ordered square).  #emph[On $cal(F)_q$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.4)")
 $ H^2 = H + cal(L)_3 #h(-0.16667em) (W_3 \( W_3^dagger W_3 - I \) W_3^dagger) +
   cal(L)_4 \( W_4 W_4^dagger \) . $ <rb:eq:square>]

]
] <rb:lem:square>

#proof[
  In normal ordering $B_p^dagger \( B_p B_r^dagger \) B_r$,
    only the two inner annihilators cross the two inner creators.
    Two, one, or zero contractions leave two-, three-, or four-body terms, respectively.
    On two particles, $H = Pi_(V_q)$ is a projection, so the two-body coefficient is $Pi_(V_q)$.
    The term with no contractions is
  $ sum_(p \, r) B \( v_p and v_r \)^dagger B \( v_p and v_r \) = cal(L)_4 \( W_4 W_4^dagger \) . $
  There is no extra factor of two: this sum, and the defining basis of $V_q ⊗ V_q$, both use ordered pairs.
    On three particles, $H = W_3 W_3^dagger$, since $W_3$ restricted to $v_p ⊗
    U_q$ is the map $B_p^dagger$ from one to three particles.
    Subtracting its two-body contribution from $H^2$ therefore determines the remaining coefficient as
  $ \( W_3 W_3^dagger \)^2 - W_3 W_3^dagger = W_3 \( W_3^dagger W_3 - I \) W_3^dagger . $
  Normal-ordered coefficients are determined successively by these restrictions, proving the identity on Fock space. ◻
]

=== 9.1 Spin coefficients and their limits <rb:sec:spin>

The three-body comparison will use the exact spin spectrum of the wedge map.
  The four-body comparison will use only finitely many coupling coefficients and their limits.

For this auxiliary calculation we simultaneously change the signs of all pair rows from (2.5), so that $v_0 = -
  e_0 and e_1$, and use the normalized lowering bases $e_p$ and $v_p$.
  This common phase change leaves $H_q$ and the intrinsic wedge maps and spin projections unchanged.
  For spin $j$, lowering from label $p$ to $p + 1$ has coefficient $sqrt(\( p + 1 \) \( 2 j - p \))$.
  Write $\( s \)_p = s \( s - 1 \) dots.h.c \( s - p + 1 \)$, with $\( s \)_0 = 1$.

#thm([Lemma 9.3.])[
  (Pair coefficients).  #emph[The coefficient of $e_i and e_j$ in $v_p$, for $i < j$, vanishes unless $i + j = p +
    1$, and otherwise equals
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.5)")
 $ v_p \( i \, j \) = \( i - j \) sqrt(frac(\( q \)_i \( q \)_j thin p !, q \( 2 q - 2 \)_p thin i ! j !)) .
   $ <rb:eq:pair>]

 For fixed $p \, i \, j$, its limit is
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.6)")
 $ v_p^(*) \( i \, j \) = \( i - j \) sqrt(frac(p !, 2^p i ! j !)) thin upright(bold(1))_(i + j = p + 1) .
   $ <rb:eq:pairstar>]

]
] <rb:lem:pair>

#proof[
  Apply simultaneous lowering $p$ times to $- e_0 and e_1$ and divide by $sqrt(\( 2 q - 2 \)_p p !)$.
    Expand the lowering operator binomially. Combining the two contributions to each increasing pair gives (9.5).
    Taking the limit gives (9.6). The normalization can also be checked directly:
    the square of the coefficient in (9.5) is
  $ \( i - j \)^2 frac(binom(q, i) binom(q, j), q binom(2 q - 2, p)) \, $
  and differentiating $\( 1 + x \)^q$ gives
  $ sum_(i \, j = 0)^q \( i - j \)^2 binom(q, i) binom(q, j) x^(i + j) = 2 q x \( 1 + x \)^(2 q - 2) . $
  Take the coefficient of $x^(p + 1)$ and halve the sum to restrict to $i < j$. ◻
]

The elementary tensor product rule decomposes spins $j_1 \, j_2$ into one copy of each spin $j_1 + j_2 - z$,
  for $0 lt.eq z lt.eq min \( 2 j_1 \, 2 j_2 \)$. Let $R_(3 \, z)$ be the corresponding projection in $V_q ⊗ U_q$,
  and put

$ d_a = 2 q - 1 \, wide d_(3 \, z) = 3 q - 1 - 2 z . $

For $q gt.eq 2$, the allowed indices are $0 lt.eq z lt.eq q$.

#thm([Lemma 9.4.])[
  (Three-body Gram eigenvalues).  #emph[
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.7)")
 $ W_3^dagger W_3 - I = sum_(z = 0)^q q_z \( q \) R_(3 \, z) \, wide
   q_z \( q \) = \( - 1 \)^z frac(\( q \)_z, \( 2 q - 2 \)_z) (3 z - 1 - frac(z \( z + 1 \), q)) . $ <rb:eq:q>]

 In particular $W_3 R_(3 \, z) = 0$ for $z = 0 \, 1 \, 3$ whenever these indices are allowed. For fixed $z$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.8)")
 $ q_z \( q \) arrow.r \( 3 z - 1 \) \( - 1 \/ 2 \)^z . $ <rb:eq:qlimit>]

]
] <rb:lem:q>

#proof[
  Identify $V_q ⊗ U_q$ with tensors antisymmetric in their first two positions.
    Then $W_3$ is $sqrt(3)$ times full antisymmetrization. Its Gram operator is the compression of $I - P_13 -
    P_23$, where $P_(i j)$ swaps tensor positions. The two swap compressions coincide,
    since conjugation by $P_12$ interchanges them and $P_12 = - I$ on the domain.
    By equivariance and multiplicity-freeness they act by the same scalar $b_z$ on each spin block.
  A highest vector of that block has coefficients $s_p$ on $v_p ⊗ e_(z - p)$, $0 lt.eq p lt.eq z$, satisfying
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.9)")
   $ s_(p + 1) / s_p = - sqrt(frac(\( z - p \) \( q - z + p + 1 \), \( p + 1 \) \( 2 q - 2 - p \))) .
     $ <rb:eq:highest-ratio>]
  This follows by applying the raising operator. Using (9.5),
    the coefficient of the compressed $P_23$ image on $v_0 ⊗ e_z$ is
  $ s_0 b_z = 1 /
    2
    [s_(z - 1) sqrt(frac(z \( q \)_z, q \( 2 q - 2 \)_(z - 1))) - s_z \( z - 1 \) sqrt(frac(\( q \)_z, \( 2 q -
    2 \)_z))] \, $
  with the first term absent for $z = 0$. Here the ordered tensor coefficients of a normalized wedge include a
    factor $1 \/ sqrt(2)$. Iteration of (9.9) gives
  $ s_z / s_0 = \( - 1 \)^z sqrt(frac(\( q \)_z, \( 2 q - 2 \)_z)) \, wide s_(z - 1) /
    s_0 = \( - 1 \)^(z - 1) sqrt(frac(z \( q - 1 \)_(z - 1), \( 2 q - 2 \)_(z - 1))) quad \( z gt.eq 1 \) . $
  Substitution gives $q_z = - 2 b_z$ in the form (9.7). The three stated null sectors have $q_z = - 1$,
    and (9.8) follows directly. ◻
]

We will repeatedly use a fixed-deficit limit of this same calculation.
  In a product of spins $j_1 \, j_2$, total lowering deficit means $T = j_1 + j_2 - m$,
  where $m$ is the total magnetic quantum number. Coefficients below are with respect to normalized monomials $X^p
  Y^(T - p) \/ sqrt(p ! \( T - p \) !)$, and are zero outside the stated index ranges.

#thm([Lemma 9.5.])[
  (Fixed-deficit coupling limit).  #emph[Suppose $j_1 \, j_2 arrow.r oo$ and $j_2 \/ \( j_1 + j_2 \) arrow.r u^2$,
    where $0 < u < 1$. Put $v = sqrt(1 - u^2)$. For fixed integers $0 lt.eq z lt.eq T$,
    the normalized coupled vector of spin $j_1 + j_2 - z$ at total lowering deficit $T$ can be chosen with real
    coefficients converging to the coefficients $s_(z \, T \, p)^(\( u \))$ of
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.10)")
 $ frac(\( u X - v Y \)^z \( v X + u Y \)^(T - z), sqrt(z ! \( T - z \) !)) . $ <rb:eq:coupling>]

 Descendants in every copy are obtained by normalized lowering,
   so the matching of bases between equal-spin copies is intertwining.]
] <rb:lem:coupling>

#proof[
  At $T = z$, the raising equation gives the adjacent ratio
  $ - sqrt(frac(\( z - p \) \( 2 j_2 - z + p + 1 \), \( p + 1 \) \( 2 j_1 - p \))) . $
  With sign $\( - 1 \)^z$ at $p = 0$, normalization yields the coefficients of $\( u X - v Y \)^z \/
    sqrt(z !)$ in the limit. The change of variables $\( X \, Y \)
    mapsto \( u X - v Y \, v X + u Y \)$ is orthogonal, so these polynomials have norm one in the
    normalized-monomial inner product. Normalized lowering from $T$ to $T +
    1$ divides the sum of the two lowering operators by
  $ sqrt(\( T - z + 1 \) { 2 \( j_1 + j_2 - z \) - \( T - z \) }) . $
  In the limit this acts by multiplication by $\( v X + u Y \) \/ sqrt(T - z + 1)$.
    Induction proves the statement for fixed descendants. ◻
]

=== 9.2 Rotational averages of explicit squares <rb:sec:squares>

We construct a positive rotational average and compare its three- and four-body terms with those of $H_q^2$.
  This section specifies the finite inequalities needed; Subsection 9.5 proves them by exact rational arithmetic.

Haar measure $d g$ on $S U \( 2 \)$ is normalized to have total mass one.
  For an operator $A$, write $A \( g \) = U \( g \) A U \( g \)^dagger$, with the appropriate Fock representation.
  Schur averaging sends a rank-one operator on an irreducible spin-$j$ space to its trace times
  $I \/ \( 2 j + 1 \)$. For several equivalent copies, it acts in the same way on the spin factor and retains the
  matrix between copies.

For each of the seven rows in Subsection 9.5, the integer $t_rho$ lies in ${ 0 \, dots.h \, 7 }$. Set

$ cal(S)_rho = { \( p \, j \) in bb(Z)^2 : 0 lt.eq p lt.eq 7 \, med 0 lt.eq j lt.eq 8 \, med 0 lt.eq p + j -
  t_rho lt.eq 8 } . $

The row specifies real coefficients $lambda_rho$ and $alpha_(p j)^rho$ for $\( p \, j \) in cal(S)_rho$;
  unlisted entries are zero. Every coefficient sum for row $rho$ below is over $cal(S)_rho$. Our comparison operator is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.11)")
 $ K = sum_(rho = 1)^7 F_rho^dagger F_rho \, wide F_rho = lambda_rho B_(t_rho) +
   sum_(\( p \, j \) in cal(S)_rho) alpha_(p j)^rho thin c_(p + j - t_rho)^dagger c_j B_p . $ <rb:eq:F>]

Thus only modes $0 \, dots.h \, 8$ occur in these auxiliary squares.

For a single row, omit $rho$ and put $i = p + j - t$, $k = p' + l - t$. Define

$ O_(p \, j \, k) = B \( v_p and e_j and e_k \) = c_k c_j B_p . $

Reordering $c_i c_k^dagger = delta_(i k) - c_k^dagger c_i$ gives the two-, three-, and four-body terms of $F^dagger F$:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.12)")
 $ & lambda^2 B_t^dagger B_t \,\
 & sum_(p \, j) lambda alpha_(p j) [\( c_i B_t \)^dagger c_j B_p + \( c_j B_p \)^dagger c_i B_t] +
   sum_(p \, j \; p' \, l) alpha_(p j) alpha_(p' l) delta_(i k) \( c_j B_p \)^dagger c_l B_(p') \,\
 & - sum_(p \, j \; p' \, l) alpha_(p j) alpha_(p' l) O_(p \, j \, k)^dagger O_(p' \, l \, i) . $ <rb:eq:normal2>]

Let $A_2 \, A_3 \, A_4$ denote these terms, summed over rows and averaged with factor $d_a$. Thus

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.13)")
 $ cal(K)_q & := d_a integral K \( g \) thin d g = A_2 + A_3 + A_4 \, wide A_2 = eta H \,\
eta & = sum_rho lambda_rho^2 = 93527408868499 / 10^14 . $ <rb:eq:Adecomp>]

==== 9.2.1 The three-body comparison <the-three-body-comparison>

Before wedging, (9.12) is a matrix on $V_q ⊗ U_q$. Its nonzero entries preserve $T = p + j$, and $T lt.eq 15$.
  Averaging therefore gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.14)")
 $ A_3 = cal(L)_3 (W_3 sum_(z = 0)^15 d_a / d_(3 \, z) e_z^q R_(3 \, z) W_3^dagger) \, $ <rb:eq:A3>]

for all sufficiently large $q$, where $e_z^q$ is the trace of the unaveraged matrix in that spin block.
  By Lemma 43, with $u = 1 \/ sqrt(3)$, these traces converge to

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.15)")
 $ e_z =
   sum_rho sum_(T = max \( z \, t_rho \))^15
   [2 lambda_rho s_(z \, T \, t_rho) sum_(p + j = T) alpha_(p j)^rho s_(z \, T \, p) + (sum_(p + j = T) alpha_(p
   j)^rho s_(z \, T \, p))^2] \, $ <rb:eq:ez>]

where $s = s^(\( 1 \/ sqrt(3) \))$ and empty inner sums are zero.
  The condition $i = k$ in (9.12) is exactly the equality of the two values of $T$.

The exact calculation in Subsection 9.5 proves

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.16)")
 $ e_z < 3 / 2 \( 3 z - 1 \) \( - 1 \/ 2 \)^z \, wide z in { 2 \, 4 \, 5 \, dots.h \, 15 } . $ <rb:eq:3certificate>]

There are finitely many strict inequalities, $d_(3 \, z) \/ d_a arrow.r 3 \/ 2$, and the omitted blocks $z = 0 \,
  1 \, 3$ are annihilated by $W_3$. Consequently, for all sufficiently large $q$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.17)")
 $ A_3 lt.eq cal(L)_3 (W_3 sum_(z = 0)^15 q_z \( q \) R_(3 \, z) W_3^dagger) . $ <rb:eq:A3bound>]

==== 9.2.2 The four-body comparison before taking limits <the-four-body-comparison-before-taking-limits>

Let

$ hat(W) : V_q ⊗ and.big^2 U_q arrow.r and.big^4 U_q $

be the wedge map. The second tensor factor decomposes into spins $q - r$, with $r$ odd, $1 lt.eq r lt.eq q$.
  In its lowering bases use the sign of Lemma 43; at $r = 1$ this is precisely the basis $v_p$.

A copy of spin $2 q - 1 - D$ is obtained by coupling $a = q - 1$ with $q - r$ at deficit $D - r$.
  Denote its vector at total deficit $T$ by $\| D \, T \; r chevron.r_q$.
  Its physical orbital-index sum is $1 + T$, and it is a highest vector when $T = D$.
  For fixed $D \, T$ and sufficiently large $q$, the allowed copy labels are

$ cal(R)_D = { 1 \, 3 \, 5 \, dots.h } ∩ \[ 1 \, D \] \, wide D lt.eq T . $

Define the real finite-flux coefficients by

$ \| D \, T \; r chevron.r_q = sum_(p + j + k = T\
j < k) S_r^q \( D \, T \; p \, j \, k \) thin v_p ⊗ \( e_j and e_k \) \, $

and extend them antisymmetrically in $j \, k$. By two applications of Lemma 43, they converge to

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.18)")
 $ S_r \( D \, T \; p \, j \, k \) = sqrt(2) thin s_(r \, j + k \, j)^(\( 1 \/ sqrt(2) \)) thin
   s_(D - r \, T - r \, p)^(\( 1 \/ sqrt(2) \)) \, $ <rb:eq:S>]

interpreted as zero if $r > j + k$. The formula holds for either ordering of $j \, k$, by antisymmetry.
  It follows by applying Lemma 43 first to the two single-particle spins and then to the two pair spins.

After averaging (9.12), its spin-$2 q - 1 - D$ block before wedging is a copy matrix $E_D^q$ times the spin
  identity, multiplied by $d_a \/ d_D$, where

$ d_D = 4 q - 1 - 2 D . $

Only $1 lt.eq D lt.eq 23$ contribute: every unaveraged term has

$ T = p + j + k = p' + l + i lt.eq 23 . $

For these finitely many $D$, entrywise convergence gives $E_D^q arrow.r E_D$, with

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.19)")
 $ \( E_D \)_(r s) = - sum_rho sum_(p \, j \; p' \, l\
T gt.eq D) alpha_(p j)^rho alpha_(p' l)^rho S_r \( D \, T \; p \, j \, k \) S_s \( D \, T \; p' \, l \, i \) \,
  $ <rb:eq:E>]

where $i = p + j - t_rho$, $k = p' + l - t_rho$, and $T = p + j + k$.
  The exact finite-flux matrix $E_D^q$ is given by the same formula with each $S_r$ replaced by $S_r^q$.

The target term $cal(L)_4 \( W_4 W_4^dagger \)$ is obtained from the $r = 1$ summand,
  since that summand is $V_q ⊗ V_q$. Thus its copy matrix, in the units just used, is $\( d_D \/ d_a \) chi$, where

$ chi_(r s) = upright(bold(1))_(r = s = 1) \, wide d_D \/ d_a arrow.r 2 . $

The summands of the target with $1 lt.eq D lt.eq 23$ equal $cal(L)_4 \( W_4 R_q^(upright("lo")) W_4^dagger \)$.
  Since $A_4$ has no other spin blocks, the comparison with $A_4$ uses only this part of the target.
  We keep $cal(L)_4 \( W_4 R_q^(upright("hi")) W_4^dagger \)$ separately.

Set

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.20)")
 $ w_(D r)^q & = hat(W) \| D \, D \; r chevron.r_q \,\
w_(D r)^(*) & = sum_(p + j + k = D\
j < k) S_r \( D \, D \; p \, j \, k \) thin v_p^(*) and e_j and e_k \, wide \( G_D \)_(r s) =
  chevron.l w_(D r)^(*) \, w_(D s)^(*) chevron.r . $ <rb:eq:wq>]

The star is a limit label, not an adjoint. The exact certificate is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.21)")
 $ G_D \( 2 chi - E_D + epsilon I \) G_D gt.eq 0 \, wide 1 lt.eq D lt.eq 23 \, wide epsilon =
   3 dot.op 10^(- 6) . $ <rb:eq:4certificate>]

We next show how these finite inequalities give the stated bound uniformly in particle number.
  Subsection 9.5 supplies their integer data and exact rational verification.

=== 9.3 A relative error bound for finite spheres <rb:sec:transfer>

The limiting Gram matrices have kernels, so their positivity cannot be transferred by assuming that their ranges
  vary continuously. Instead, we compare the annihilators themselves.
  An exact diagonal change of variables reduces every error to a fixed finite family of pair-generated
  annihilators. This gives an error relative to $H_q$, uniformly on the full Fock space.

For the local argument, let $cal(F)_(upright("loc"))$ be the exterior algebra on the 25 orthonormal modes $e_0 \,
  dots.h \, e_24$. For $q gt.eq 24$ we identify it with the Fock space of the first 25 orbital modes in $U_q$.
  By (9.6), each limiting pair vector $v_p^(*)$ with $p lt.eq 23$ lies in $and.big^2 bb(C)^25$.
  Contraction by these vectors is defined on $cal(F)_(upright("loc"))$ by the same exterior-algebra convention as
  before.

#thm([Lemma 9.6.])[
  (Compression and annihilator bounds).  #emph[Let $W$ be a linear map between finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces,
    $G = W^dagger W$, and let $C$ be self-adjoint on the domain of $W$. Then
$ G C G gt.eq 0 quad arrow.l.r.double quad W C W^dagger gt.eq 0 . $

 On $cal(F)_(upright("loc"))$, for any fixed finite collection $A_b = c_k c_j B \( v_p^(*) \)$ with $0 lt.eq
   p lt.eq 23$ and $0 lt.eq j \, k lt.eq 24$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.22)")
 $ sum_b lr(bar.v.double A_b psi bar.v.double)^2 lt.eq C_0 chevron.l psi \, h_(*) psi chevron.r \, wide h_(*) :=
   sum_(p = 0)^23 B \( v_p^(*) \)^dagger B \( v_p^(*) \) \, $ <rb:eq:annihilatorbound>]

 where $C_0$ depends only on the collection.]
] <rb:lem:compression>

#proof[
  The two positivity statements both assert that the quadratic form of $C$ is nonnegative on $"ran" G =
    "ran" W^dagger$. For the second assertion, use $lr(bar.v.double c_k c_j B \( v_p^(*) \) psi bar.v.double)
    lt.eq lr(bar.v.double B \( v_p^(*) \) psi bar.v.double)$ and sum;
    one may take $C_0$ to be the largest multiplicity of a pair label $p$. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 9.7.])[
  (Uniform transfer).  #emph[For $q gt.eq 24$ and $1 lt.eq D lt.eq 23$, put
$ C_D^q = \( d_D \/ d_a \) chi - E_D^q + epsilon I \, wide h_q = sum_(p = 0)^23 B_p^dagger B_p . $

 Assuming (9.21), there is a scalar $rho_q gt.eq 0$, independent of $D$ and tending to zero as $q arrow.r oo$, such that
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.23)")
 $ sum_(r \, s in cal(R)_D) \( C_D^q \)_(r s) B \( w_(D r)^q \)^dagger B \( w_(D s)^q \) gt.eq -
   rho_q h_q $ <rb:eq:relative>]

 on the entire Fock space $cal(F)_q$.]
] <rb:lem:transfer>

#proof[
  #emph[An exact change of variables.] Take $q gt.eq 24$. All annihilators in (9.23), including those in $h_q$,
    involve only the modes $0 \, dots.h \, 24$. First work on $cal(F)_(upright("loc"))$.
    Define the positive diagonal operator
  $ Delta_q \| A chevron.r = (product_(x in A) d_q \( x \)) \| A chevron.r \, wide d_q \( x \) =
    sqrt(\( q \)_x \/ q^x) \, $
  on each basis wedge $\| A chevron.r$. This local Fock space is fixed as $q$ varies.
    Thus $Delta_q$ and $Delta_q^(- 1)$ converge in norm to $I$; also $0 < Delta_q lt.eq I$.
  The pair coefficient formula (9.5) gives
  $ v_p \( x \, y \) = r_p \( q \) d_q \( x \) d_q \( y \) v_p^(*) \( x \, y \) \, wide r_p \( q \) =
    sqrt(\( 2 q \)^p \/ \( 2 q - 2 \)_p) gt.eq 1 . $
  Checking the factors attached to the removed orbital labels on a basis wedge gives the exact identities
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.24)")
   $ B_p & = r_p \( q \) Delta_q^(- 1) B \( v_p^(*) \) Delta_q \,\
B \( v_p and e_j and e_k \) & = frac(r_p \( q \), d_q \( j \) d_q \( k \)) thin Delta_q^(- 1)
  B \( v_p^(*) and e_j and e_k \) Delta_q . $ <rb:eq:pairconjugation>]
  These are identities on the local Fock space, with the inverse diagonal acting on the output particle sector.
    Repeated removed labels make both sides zero. In particular,
    solving (9.24) for $B \( v_p^(*) \) Delta_q$ and using $r_p \( q \) gt.eq
    1$ and $lr(bar.v.double Delta_q bar.v.double) lt.eq 1$ gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.25)")
   $ Delta_q h_(*) Delta_q lt.eq h_q . $ <rb:eq:pairenergytransfer>]
  #emph[The error is controlled by pair energy.] Fix $D$. Index a finite family by $b = \( p \, j \, k \)$ with
    $p + j + k = D$ and $j < k$, and put
  $ A_b = B \( v_p^(*) and e_j and e_k \) \, wide beta_(r b)^q =
    S_r^q \( D \, D \; p \, j \, k \) frac(r_p \( q \), d_q \( j \) d_q \( k \)) . $
  Equations (9.20) and (9.24) yield
  $ B \( w_(D r)^q \) = Delta_q^(- 1) (sum_b beta_(r b)^q A_b) Delta_q . $
  The coefficients are real, and Lemma 43 gives $beta_(r b)^q arrow.r beta_(r b)^(*) :=
    S_r \( D \, D \; p \, j \, k \)$. Hence the finite real symmetric matrices
  $ M_q = \( beta^q \)^T C_D^q beta^q quad upright("converge to") quad
    M_(*) = \( beta^(*) \)^T \( 2 chi - E_D + epsilon I \) beta^(*) . $
  The quadratic expression in (9.23) is consequently
  $ Delta_q (sum_(b \, c) \( M_q \)_(b c) A_b^dagger Delta_q^(- 2) A_c) Delta_q . $
  Its limiting expression before the outer diagonals is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.26)")
   $ sum_(b \, c) \( M_(*) \)_(b c) A_b^dagger A_c = sum_(r \, s) \( 2 chi - E_D + epsilon I \)_(r s)
     B \( w_(D r)^(*) \)^dagger B \( w_(D s)^(*) \) gt.eq 0 . $ <rb:eq:limitpositive>]
  Indeed, the certificate (9.21) and Lemma 44, applied to the map with columns $w_(D r)^(*)$,
    give positivity before lifting; the lift $cal(L)_4$ preserves positivity.
  For clarity, the perturbation estimate does not require $M_(*) gt.eq 0$. Let $R_q$ be the block matrix with entries
  $ \( R_q \)_(b c) = \( M_q \)_(b c) Delta_q^(- 2) - \( M_(*) \)_(b c) I . $
  Its norm tends to zero, since both its index set and the local Fock space are fixed. For every vector $phi.alt$,
  $ lr(|sum_(b \, c) chevron.l A_b phi.alt \, \( R_q \)_(b c) A_c phi.alt chevron.r|) lt.eq
    lr(bar.v.double R_q bar.v.double) sum_b lr(bar.v.double A_b phi.alt bar.v.double)^2 lt.eq
    C_0 lr(bar.v.double R_q bar.v.double) chevron.l phi.alt \, h_(*) phi.alt chevron.r . $
  Combine this estimate with (9.26), take $phi.alt = Delta_q psi$, and apply (9.25).
    This proves (9.23) on the local Fock space with a coefficient tending to zero.
    Taking a maximum over the fixed set $1 lt.eq D lt.eq 23$ gives a common $rho_q$.
  #emph[Additional particles are spectators.] Order the 25 local modes before all other modes in the Fock
    factorization. Every operator in (9.23) acts on the local factor alone.
    Tensoring the local inequality with the identity proves it on $cal(F)_q$, with the same $rho_q$,
    regardless of the number of occupied modes outside this factor. ◻
]

The allowance $epsilon I$ in the certificate must also be paid for in pair energy.
  Here it is useful to sum over the orthonormal highest vectors #emph[before] estimating their wedges.
  Bessel’s inequality then avoids an extra factor equal to the number of spin copies.

#thm([Proposition 9.8.])[
  (Four-body estimate).  #emph[For the coefficients in Subsection 9.5,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.27)")
 $ A_4 lt.eq cal(L)_4 \( W_4 R_q^(upright("lo")) W_4^dagger \) + \( 1222 epsilon + o \( 1 \) \) H \, wide
   q arrow.r oo \, $ <rb:eq:A4bound>]

 uniformly on $cal(F)_q$.]
] <rb:prop:A4>

#proof[
  Haar averaging the matrix unit $\| D \, D \; r chevron.r_q chevron.l D \, D \;
    s \|_q$ gives the corresponding copy matrix unit tensored with the spin identity, divided by $d_D$.
    Therefore, on the blocks $1 lt.eq D lt.eq 23$, the low-block target minus $A_4$,
    with the $epsilon I$ allowance added, is
  $ d_a sum_(D = 1)^23 integral sum_(r \, s) \( C_D^q \)_(r s) B \( w_(D r)^q \( g \) \)^dagger
    B \( w_(D s)^q \( g \) \) thin d g . $
  Lemma 45, conjugated by each rotation, bounds this below by $- o \( 1 \) H$, because Schur averaging gives
  $ d_a integral h_q \( g \) thin d g = 24 H . $
  The number of blocks in the comparison is fixed, so this error remains uniform in particle number.
  To bound the allowance, fix $D$ and write
  $ \| D \, D \; r chevron.r_q = sum_(p + j + k = D\
j < k) S_(r b)^q thin v_p ⊗ \( e_j and e_k \) \, wide b = \( p \, j \, k \) . $
  The uncoupled vectors on the right are orthonormal. So are the highest vectors on the left:
    distinct $r$ belong to orthogonal spin summands of the second pair factor. Thus $S^q \( S^q \)^T = I$.
    Apply the contraction $S^q ⊗ I$ to the Hilbert-space-valued vector $\( c_k c_j B_p psi \)_b$. It follows that
  $ sum_(r in cal(R)_D) lr(bar.v.double B \( w_(D r)^q \) psi bar.v.double)^2 lt.eq sum_(p + j + k = D\
j < k) lr(bar.v.double c_k c_j B_p psi bar.v.double)^2 lt.eq sum_(p + j + k = D\
j < k) lr(bar.v.double B_p psi bar.v.double)^2 . $
  This uses orthogonality before wedging; the vectors $w_(D r)^q$ themselves need not be orthogonal.
    The number of triples on the right is
  $ n_D = sum_(n = 1)^D ⌈n / 2⌉ = ⌊frac(\( D + 1 \)^2, 4)⌋ . $
  Conjugate by rotations and integrate. Each pair norm contributes $chevron.l psi \, H psi chevron.r \/ d_a$,
    so the total allowance is bounded by
  $ epsilon sum_(D = 1)^23 n_D H = epsilon (sum_(m = 1)^12 m^2 + sum_(m = 1)^11 m \( m + 1 \)) H = 1222 epsilon H . $
  Removing the allowance proves (9.27). No block of $R_q^(upright("hi"))$ has entered this estimate. ◻
]

=== 9.4 The three-body tail and the retained-block inequality <rb:sec:finish>

The preceding comparison has used only the low four-body target.
  It remains to control the three-body blocks above $z = 15$ by pair energy and combine the estimates.

#thm([Lemma 9.9.])[
  (Three-body tail).  #emph[For sufficiently large $q$,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.28)")
 $ cal(L)_3 (W_3 sum_(z = 16)^q q_z \( q \) R_(3 \, z) W_3^dagger) gt.eq - delta_q H \, $ <rb:eq:tailbound>]

 where
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.29)")
 $ delta_q = sum_(17 lt.eq z lt.eq q\
z upright(" odd")) d_(3 \, z) / d_a \| q_z \( q \) \| \( z + 1 \) \, wide delta_q arrow.r 61 / 4096 . $ <rb:eq:delta>]

]
] <rb:lem:tail>

#proof[
  For $16 lt.eq z lt.eq q$, the bracket in (9.7) is positive, since it is at least $2 z - 2$.
    Thus only odd $z$ contribute negatively. A normalized highest vector of $R_(3 \, z)$ is $sum_(p = 0)^z s_p
    v_p ⊗ e_(z - p)$. Its orbit resolves that spin projector with factor $d_(3 \, z)$.
    After wedging and lifting, its quadratic form is
  $ lr(bar.v.double sum_(p = 0)^z s_p c_(z - p) \( g \) B_p \( g \) psi bar.v.double)^2 lt.eq
    sum_(p = 0)^z lr(bar.v.double B_p \( g \) psi bar.v.double)^2 . $
  Averaging each summand proves (9.28) with (9.29).
  For $q gt.eq 4$, the ratios in $\( q \)_z \/ \( 2 q - 2 \)_z$ are nonincreasing, so
  $ frac(\( q \)_z, \( 2 q - 2 \)_z) lt.eq (frac(q, 2 q - 2))^z lt.eq \( 2 \/ 3 \)^z \, wide d_(3 \, z) /
    d_a lt.eq 2 . $
  The summands, extended by zero for $z > q$, are therefore dominated by $6 z \( z + 1 \) \( 2 \/ 3 \)^z$.
    Dominated convergence and (9.8) give
  $ lim_(q arrow.r oo) delta_q = 3 / 2 sum_(z = 17 \, 19 \, dots.h) \( 3 z - 1 \) \( z + 1 \) 2^(- z) = 61 / 4096 . $
  The last identity follows by writing $z = 17 + 2 m$ and using the geometric-series identities for $sum t^m$,
    $sum m t^m$, and $sum m^2 t^m$ at $t = 1 \/ 4$. ◻
]

#proof[
  Write $T_(upright("lo")) = cal(L)_4 \( W_4 R_q^(upright("lo")) W_4^dagger \)$ and $T_(upright("hi")) =
    cal(L)_4 \( W_4 R_q^(upright("hi")) W_4^dagger \)$. The normal-square identity,
    the three-body comparison (9.17), and the tail estimate give
  $ H^2 gt.eq \( 1 - delta_q \) H + A_3 + T_(upright("lo")) + T_(upright("hi")) . $
  By (9.27), $T_(upright("lo")) gt.eq A_4 - \( 1222 epsilon + o \( 1 \) \) H$.
    Using $cal(K)_q = eta H + A_3 + A_4 gt.eq 0$ from (9.13), we obtain
  $ H^2 gt.eq cal(K)_q + \( 1 - eta - delta_q - 1222 epsilon - o \( 1 \) \) H + T_(upright("hi")) . $
  The coefficient of $H$ tends to
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.30)")
   $ 1 - 93527408868499 / 10^14 - 61 / 4096 - 1222 3 / 10^6 = 4616733319001 / 10^14 > 1 / 25 . $ <rb:eq:finalmargin>]
  For fixed $0 < c_0 < 1 \/ 25$, choose $q$ large enough that this coefficient exceeds $c_0$ and discard the
    positive operator $cal(K)_q$. This is (9.3). The matrix comparisons involved finitely many fixed orbital
    modes, the transfer estimates held on full Fock space, and the tail estimate was uniform in particle number.
    Therefore the same threshold applies simultaneously to every sector. ◻
]

=== 9.5 The finite rational certificate <rb:app:certificate>

We finish by verifying the two finite inequalities (9.16) and (9.21) used above.
  The seven integer rows and all rational formulas are given here.
  Starting from the integer rows in Subsection 9.5.1, evaluate the rational three-body traces in Subsection 9.5.2
  and the rational four-body matrices in Subsection 9.5.3. The margin and coefficient-sign tables in those
  sections record the resulting checks. The formulas below determine every value using only integers and rational
  numbers.

==== 9.5.1 Row data <rb:app:rows>

Let $P = 10^7$. The first two numbers of each row are $t$ and $ell = P lambda$.
  An entry $p j : a$ specifies the integer $a_(p j)$, with $p$ and $j$ parsed as separate single-digit labels;
  omitted entries are zero. Continuation lines belong to the preceding row. Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.31)")
 $ alpha_(p j) = a_(p j) / P sqrt(frac(2^p binom(p + j, p), 2^t binom(p + j, t))) = a_(p j) /
   P sqrt(frac(2^(p - t) t ! \( p + j - t \) !, p ! j !)) . $ <rb:eq:alphadata>]

Every nonzero entry has $0 lt.eq p + j - t lt.eq 8$.

#raw(block: true, lang: "", "")

In particular $P^2 eta = sum_rho ell_rho^2 = 93527408868499$.

==== 9.5.2 Three-body arithmetic <rb:app:three-arithmetic>

For $c in { 1 \, 2 }$, an integer $T gt.eq 0$, and integers $z \, p$, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.32)")
 $ sans(U) \( c \, z \, T \, p \) = sum_(h = max \( 0 \, p + z - T \))^(min \( p \, z \)) \( - 1 \)^(z - h)
   binom(z, h) binom(T - z, p - h) c^(p - h) \, $ <rb:eq:U>]

and set it to zero if $z$ or $p$ lies outside $\[ 0 \, T \]$. Expanding (9.10) gives

$ s_(z \, T \, p)^(\( 1 \/ sqrt(3) \)) = sans(U) \( 2 \, z \, T \, p \)
  sqrt(frac(2^z binom(T, z), 3^T 2^p binom(T, p))) . $

Substitution in (9.15) and (9.31) cancels all radicals:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.33)")
 $ P^2 e_z = sum_rho sum_(T = max \( z \, t_rho \))^15 frac(2^z binom(T, z), 3^T 2^(t_rho) binom(T, t_rho))
   X_(rho \, T) (X_(rho \, T) + 2 ell_rho sans(U) \( 2 \, z \, T \, t_rho \)) \, $ <rb:eq:erational>]

where

$ X_(rho \, T) = sum_(p + j = T) a_(p j)^rho sans(U) \( 2 \, z \, T \, p \) \, $

with value zero on empty levels. For each $z in { 2 \, 4 \, 5 \, dots.h \, 15 }$,
  evaluate (9.33) and form the exact rational margin $m_z = 3 / 2 \( 3 z - 1 \) \( - 1 \/ 2 \)^z - e_z$.
  Write $10^6 m_z = n_z \/ d_z$ with integers $n_z \, d_z$ and $d_z > 0$.
