==== 9.5.3 Four-body arithmetic <rb:app:four-arithmetic>

Fix $1 lt.eq D lt.eq 23$; all copy indices below lie in $cal(R)_D$. Put

$ u_r = frac(1, r ! \( D - r \) !) \, wide \( E_D \)_(r s) = sqrt(u_r u_s) thin Y_(r s) \, wide \( G_D \)_(r s) =
  sqrt(u_r u_s) thin Z_(r s) . $

The matrices $Y \, Z$ are rational. Define

$ sans(V)_r \( D \, T \; p \, j \, k \) = sans(U) \( 1 \, r \, j + k \, j \) sans(U) \( 1 \, D - r \, T - r \, p \) \, $

with value zero for $r > j + k$. Two applications of (9.10) give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.34)")
 $ S_r \( D \, T \; p \, j \, k \) = sans(V)_r \( D \, T \; p \, j \, k \)
   sqrt(2^(1 - \( j + k \) - T + r) frac(j ! k ! p ! thin u_r, \( T - D \) !)) . $ <rb:eq:Srational>]

Therefore

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.35)")
 $ P^2 Y_(r s) = - sum_rho sum_(p \, j \; p' \, l\
T gt.eq D) & a_(p j)^rho a_(p' l)^rho thin sans(V)_r \( D \, T \; p \, j \, k \) sans(V)_s \( D \, T \; p' \, l \, i \)\
 & times 2^(1 - 2 T + p + p' - t_rho + \( r + s \) \/ 2) frac(i ! k ! t_rho !, \( T - D \) !) \, $ <rb:eq:Yrational>]

where $i = p + j - t_rho$, $k = p' + l - t_rho$, and $T = p + j + k$. Both entry sums are ordered.
  To verify the cancellation, multiply the two factors in (9.34) by the two factors (9.31);
  their remaining square root is exactly the factorial and power-of-two factor displayed in (9.35).

For an increasing quadruple $A$ of nonnegative indices with sum $D + 1$, put $f \( A \) = product_(b in A) b !$.
  If $\( x \, y \, j \, k \)$ lists its elements, let $sigma \( x \, y \, j \, k \)$ be the sign of sorting this
  list into increasing order. In each summand below put $p = x + y - 1$, and define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.36)")
 $ L_r \( A \) = 2^(\( 1 - 2 D + r \) \/ 2) sum_(x < y upright(" in ") A\
{ j \, k } = A \\ { x \, y } \, med j < k) sigma \( x \, y \, j \, k \) \( x - y \) thin p ! j ! k ! thin
  sans(V)_r \( D \, D \; p \, j \, k \) . $ <rb:eq:Lrational>]

Combining (9.6) and (9.34), the coefficient of $w_(D r)^(*)$ on the Slater wedge $A$ is $sqrt(u_r \/ f \( A \))
  L_r \( A \)$. Thus

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.37)")
 $ Z_(r s) = sum_(A upright(" increasing quadruple")\
sum_(b in A) b = D + 1) frac(L_r \( A \) L_s \( A \), f \( A \)) . $ <rb:eq:Zrational>]

All exponents of two in these formulas are integers, since $r \, s$ are odd.

Let $D_u = "diag" \( u_r \)$ and $D_(sqrt(u)) = "diag" \( sqrt(u_r) \)$.
  The matrix in (9.21) equals $D_(sqrt(u)) M D_(sqrt(u))$, where

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.38)")
 $ M = Z B Z \, wide B = "diag" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( 2 upright(bold(1))_(r = 1) + epsilon \) u_r
   #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] - D_u Y D_u . $ <rb:eq:M>]

Thus it suffices to check $M gt.eq 0$ using rational arithmetic.

To check positivity for each $D = 1 \, dots.h \, 23$, form $Y$ and $Z$ from (9.35) and (9.37),
  then form $B$ and $M$ from (9.38), using $epsilon = 3 \/ 10^6$.
  All sums range over the printed rows and the finite index sets specified above.
  Compute with exact fractions throughout. If $d = \| cal(R)_D \|$, initialize $J = I_d$.
  For $b = 1 \, dots.h \, d$, compute in order

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(9.39)")
 $ X = M J \, wide c_b = frac("tr" X, b) \, wide J = c_b I_d - X . $ <rb:eq:recurrence>]

Then $det \( x I + M \) = x^d + c_1 x^(d - 1) + dots.h.c + c_d$.
